var e=[{week:1,unitId:`character`,title:`Volunteering — Time, Talent, and Energy`,focus:`Community contribution`,outcomeFocus:`Volunteering is donating time, talent, and energy to benefit people and community. It can build purpose and belonging.`,materials:[`Printed Week 1 worksheet or this page`,`Board: Time / Talent / Energy`,`Timer`],knowledge:[`Volunteering is donating time, talent, and energy to benefit people, communities, or the environment.`,`Volunteering is a choice to contribute — not a chore and not a punishment.`],understanding:[`Connecting knowledge and skills to volunteering can support personal and community well-being.`],skills:[`Connect knowledge and skills to volunteering opportunities.`],warmUp:`Ask: “When has someone given their time to help you — a coach, a library volunteer, a lunch supervisor, a family member?” Two students share. Write Time, Talent, Energy on the board.`,teachTitle:`What volunteering is (and is not)`,teachSteps:[`Volunteering means you give time, talent, or energy to help people or a place — not because you are paid, but because it helps.`,`Time = hours you show up (shelving books, picking litter). Talent = a skill you share (reading with a buddy, drawing a poster). Energy = effort when the job is ordinary (stacking chairs, packing food hampers).`,`It is not the same as a chore you must do at home, and it is not a punishment. It is a choice to contribute.`,`Grade 5 examples that fit our community: school recycling team, reading buddy, helping at a community league event, packing kits for a food bank drive, walking dogs at a shelter with a family adult.`,`Helping can grow a sense of purpose (“I am useful here”) and belonging (“I am part of this group”). One regular, honest contribution is enough to start.`],story:`On Thursday afternoon, Maya waited in the hallway while most of her classmates hurried toward the buses. Ms. Patel was standing beside the lost-and-found bin, which was overflowing with hoodies, water bottles, and mittens that smelled like wet snow.

"Could you use an extra pair of hands?" Ms. Patel asked.

Maya almost said she had to leave. Her friends were saving her a seat on the bus, and she had promised to text her grandmother when she started home. Then she noticed a small Grade 3 student searching through the pile with worried eyes.

Maya decided she could stay for twenty minutes. She folded the hoodies, matched mitten pairs, and lined up the water bottles. She was good at spotting patterns, so she made three neat sections: clothing, drink containers, and things that needed a name tag. Ms. Patel found a printer, and Maya made a sign that said, Please take what is yours.

The work was not exciting. The bin smelled like wet mittens, and Maya's arms grew tired. When her friends waved from the bus loop, she kept sorting until the last sleeve was folded.

The next morning, Liam from Grade 3 found his blue toque. He pulled it on and grinned as if someone had handed him a present. Maya felt pleased, but she did not pretend she had fixed every problem in the school. She had simply noticed a need and offered her time, talent, and energy. Volunteering was a choice to contribute, not a punishment or a chore someone forced on her. The hallway felt calmer, and the lost-and-found was finally usable.`,activity:`Students complete the volunteer map. They do not need a real placement today — they connect skills they already have to a possible contribution. If a student says “I have no talents,” name one you have seen (holds the door, explains math, notices when someone is left out).`,worksheetTitle:`My volunteer map`,fields:[{kind:`intro`,text:`Volunteering = giving time, talent, or energy to help people or community.`},{kind:`text`,id:`skill`,label:`1. One skill or strength I already have`},{kind:`text`,id:`who`,label:`2. Who or what could that help? (school, family, community, land, animals)`},{kind:`table`,id:`idea`,columns:[`What I would do`,`Time, talent, or energy?`,`Who benefits`],rows:1},{kind:`text`,id:`barrier`,label:`4. One thing that might get in the way (transport, shyness, time) and one way around it`,rows:2},{kind:`exit`,id:`exit`,prompt:`In one sentence: How could volunteering support your well-being or someone else’s?`}],sample:{skill:`I am good at organizing bins and making clear signs. I notice when the lost-and-found is a mess.`,who:`Our school hallway and younger students who lose mittens. Also the lunch supervisors who currently have to dig through the pile.`,idea:`Stay 20 minutes Thursdays to fold lost-and-found and make a claim-your-stuff sign | Time + talent (organizing) + energy | Grade 3s, office staff, anyone who lost a hat`,barrier:`I might miss the late bus. I can ask if I can start at 3:05 and still catch the 3:25 bus, or do it at lunch on Friday instead.`,exit:`Helping the lost-and-found makes me feel useful, and younger kids feel looked after when they get their hats back.`},checks:[`Can the student define volunteering in their own words (time, talent, or energy — not pay)?`,`Did they name one realistic Grade 5 contribution?`,`Can they say who would benefit?`],tip:`Keep ideas local and doable. Avoid “save the world” lists. School jobs count: recycling, younger-buddy reading, set-up crew. Never require unpaid labour as a grade.`,preview:`Next week we look at life experiences — the stories that shape how we see challenges.`},{week:2,unitId:`character`,title:`Life Experiences Shape Who We Are`,focus:`Experiences and understanding`,outcomeFocus:`Life experiences can inform strengths and preferences. They shape how we understand events and how we respond.`,materials:[`Week 2 worksheet`,`Board: Experience → What I learned → How I respond`],knowledge:[`Life experiences can be individual or shared.`,`Life experiences can inform strengths, skills, and preferences.`],understanding:[`Life experiences can shape an individual's understanding of events and how they respond.`],skills:[`Examine how life experiences can shape an individual's understanding of events.`],warmUp:`Read: “Two students both miss the same soccer goal. One laughs and tries again. One sits out.” Ask: What life experiences might sit behind those two responses? Take two ideas. No names, no roasting.`,teachTitle:`Experiences leave traces`,teachSteps:[`A life experience is something that happens to you or that you take part in: a move, a new school, a sport, a loss, a success, a family tradition, a tough test, a kind teacher.`,`Experiences can be individual (you practised piano alone) or shared (your class put on a concert).`,`Over time, experiences can teach skills, strengths, and preferences (“I like working with my hands”; “I need quiet before a test”).`,`They can also shape how we read a new situation. If you have been left out before, a group project can feel risky. If you have been coached well, a hard skill can feel possible.`],story:`Noah kept a small card in the back of his agenda. On it, he had written, A warm-up helps me think. He did not show it to everyone, but he looked at it whenever his stomach tightened before speaking in front of the class.

At his old school in Sherwood Park, Noah had entered a spelling bee. He had practised a simple word, but when the microphone reached him, his mind went blank. He sat down shaking while the next student spelled the word correctly. Noah remembered the hot feeling in his face even a year later.

At his new school, partners practised out loud before Friday quizzes and presentations. Noah still felt nervous, but he learned to ask for rehearsal time instead of pretending everything was easy. One afternoon, his intramural soccer team missed a wide shot. A teammate groaned and walked toward the bench. Noah recognized that look.

"Want to try it once with me?" he asked. They moved closer to the net, talked through where to aim, and tried again. The second shot was not perfect, but it went much closer. Noah realized that his spelling-bee experience had left him with more than a bad memory. It had taught him what support could feel like and helped him notice when someone else needed it.

Later, the class made private maps of experiences, skills, and preferences. Noah wrote about the spelling bee, asking for practice, and liking a quiet rehearsal before being put on the spot. He did not have to share the whole story. Saying, "I do better after a warm-up," was enough. A move, a missed chance, or learning to ride a bike on a gravel alley can leave traces too. Our experiences help shape how we understand ourselves and how we respond to new moments.`,activity:`Students complete a private experience map. Tell them: you will not read these aloud. Share only if you choose the last box. Protect privacy — no forcing trauma stories. Everyday examples are enough (learning to ride a bike, joining choir, moving houses).`,worksheetTitle:`My experience map (private)`,fields:[{kind:`intro`,text:`Use school-appropriate examples. You do not have to write about anything painful. Everyday experiences count.`},{kind:`table`,id:`exp`,columns:[`The experience`,`Skill or strength it built`],rows:1},{kind:`text`,id:`pref`,label:`2. One experience that shaped a preference (how I like to learn, play, or rest)`,rows:2},{kind:`text`,id:`respond`,label:`3. How might that experience change the way I respond when something similar happens again?`,rows:2},{kind:`text`,id:`share`,label:`4. Optional to share with a partner: one sentence I am willing to say out loud`},{kind:`exit`,id:`exit`,prompt:`Finish: Life experiences can shape our understanding because ________.`}],sample:{exp:`Freezing in a spelling bee at my old school, then practising out loud here | Asking for a warm-up instead of pretending I am fine`,pref:`I like a quiet rehearsal with a partner before I have to perform in front of the class.`,respond:`If I feel panic before a quiz or a game, I will ask for practise time instead of shutting down or sitting out.`,share:`I like a warm-up before I perform.`,exit:`they teach us skills and how we read a new situation, like asking for practise after a freeze.`},checks:[`Did the student name an experience and a skill or preference that came from it?`,`Can they explain, in one line, that experiences influence how we respond?`,`Did they keep classmates’ stories private?`],tip:`Model a mild personal example first. If a student writes something that sounds like a safety disclosure, follow EIPS reporting — do not put it under the document camera.`,preview:`Next week: perseverance — staying with hard things without fake cheerfulness.`},{week:3,unitId:`character`,title:`Perseverance — Staying With Hard Things`,focus:`Effort and well-being`,outcomeFocus:`Perseverance is finding ways to continue to improve skills and stay motivated during difficult situations. It includes effort, courage, commitment, and belief.`,materials:[`Week 3 worksheet`,`Board: Effort · Courage · Commitment · Belief`],knowledge:[`Perseverance is finding ways to continue to improve skills and stay motivated during difficult situations.`,`Perseverance includes effort, courage, commitment, and belief.`],understanding:[`Perseverance can lead to positive feelings associated with achieving a goal, for individuals and communities. Rest is part of the plan, not quitting.`],skills:[`Describe how perseverance can affect well-being.`],warmUp:`Show a 10-second “first try vs later try” story (tying shoes, a math strategy, a jump rope trick). Ask: What did the person do between try one and try ten? List verbs: practised, asked for help, rested, tried a new way.`,teachTitle:`Name perseverance without fake cheerfulness`,teachSteps:[`Perseverance is not “never feel stuck.” It is staying with a hard thing long enough to improve — sometimes by changing the plan.`,`Four supports: effort (you show up), courage (you try while nervous), commitment (you return after a break), belief (you think improvement is possible).`,`Perseverance can be individual (you keep decoding a book) or group/community (a class fundraiser that takes weeks).`,`It can lead to positive feelings when a goal moves forward. It can also be tiring. Rest is part of the plan, not quitting.`],story:`Aisha's recorder made a squeak so loud that two students in the hallway turned around. The high B in her band piece sounded less like a musical note and more like a surprised duck. Band started in twelve minutes, and Aisha considered putting the recorder in her backpack and telling Mr. Kwan that her throat hurt.

Instead, she sat on the edge of the stage and opened her music book. She played the difficult bar slowly, then even more slowly. When Mr. Kwan passed by, she asked whether she could sit in the second row so she could watch his fingers during rehearsal. He showed her a fingering chart and helped her circle the exact place where her thumb was slipping.

At lunch, Aisha practised for six extra minutes. Then she played the squeaky note for two friends. They laughed kindly, not because she was foolish, but because the sound really was funny. Aisha laughed too and tried again.

That evening, her jaw felt tired, so she stopped after a short practice and rested. In her agenda she wrote, High B gets cleaner if I slow down. On Thursday, she came back to band ready to work, even though she still did not know whether the note would behave.

By Friday, the high B was not perfect. It was simply less of a duck. Aisha did not make a heroic announcement. She told the class that perseverance meant staying with a hard thing long enough to improve, sometimes by changing the plan instead of pretending the task was easy. Asking for a better seat, practising in small pieces, and resting when her jaw needed a break had all helped. The note had improved because Aisha kept returning to it with a realistic plan.`,activity:`Students pick one real hard thing from school, sport, music, friendship, or a chore. They map what perseverance could look like this week — not a hero speech.`,worksheetTitle:`Perseverance plan (one hard thing)`,fields:[{kind:`text`,id:`hard`,label:`The hard thing (school-appropriate)`},{kind:`table`,id:`supports`,columns:[`Support`,`What this could look like for me this week`],rowLabels:[`Effort`,`Courage`,`Commitment (including rest)`,`Belief I can improve`]},{kind:`text`,id:`help`,label:`One person or tool that could help (not “try harder”)`},{kind:`exit`,id:`exit`,prompt:`How could perseverance on this thing support your well-being? (mood, confidence, health, belonging)`}],sample:{hard:`Getting a clean high B on recorder before band on Fridays.`,supports:`Effort: six extra minutes at lunch with the fingering chart. | Courage: play the squeaky note in front of two friends. | Commitment: rest my jaw, come back Thursday. | Belief: High B gets cleaner if I slow down.`,help:`Mr. Kwan’s fingering chart taped inside the case, and sitting in the second row to watch his fingers.`,exit:`I dread band less, I feel more able, and I still belong in the group even when the note is not perfect yet.`},checks:[`Can the student tell perseverance from “pretend it is easy”?`,`Did they name a concrete action, not only “don’t give up”?`,`Did they include a help source or a rest plan?`],tip:`Watch for students who confuse perseverance with never asking for help. Praise “I tried a new strategy” more than “I suffered longer.”`,preview:`Next week we connect skills to volunteering and community.`},{week:4,unitId:`character`,title:`Strengths, Skills, and Community Contribution`,focus:`From me to we`,outcomeFocus:`Every individual has the ability to contribute in different ways. Connect personal knowledge and skills to volunteering opportunities.`,materials:[`Week 4 worksheet`,`Optional: list of real school volunteer jobs`],knowledge:[`Communities need many kinds of contribution, not only loud leadership.`,`Life experiences are clues to where a skill can help.`],understanding:[`Connecting knowledge and skills to volunteering opportunities can support personal and community well-being.`],skills:[`Connect knowledge and skills to volunteering opportunities.`],warmUp:`Rapid list on the board: quiet strengths (notices litter, remembers names, good with younger kids, patient with tech). Students add two more. Point out: loud leadership is only one kind.`,teachTitle:`Match skills to a real need`,teachSteps:[`Communities need many kinds of contribution: organizers, greeters, lifters, artists, translators, people who stay after to put things away.`,`Your life experiences (Weeks 1–3) are clues. If you have moved schools, you know how a new student feels. If you play an instrument, you can help at a concert set-up.`,`A good match is useful and realistic. “Become famous and donate millions” is not a Grade 5 plan. “Help the office stuff envelopes on Friday” is.`,`Contribution can be short (one event) or ongoing (a weekly job). Both count.`],story:`Liam did not enjoy grabbing a microphone or standing in front of the whole school. He was better at remembering names and noticing small details. He knew which classroom door squeaked, which hook was empty, and who usually ate lunch alone.

When Priya arrived from another Elk Island school, the building seemed to swallow her. She stood beside the boot rack holding her backpack straps tightly. Liam walked over and introduced himself. He showed her where to leave wet boots, which staircase led to the library, and the quiet side of the playground where students could sit when the field felt too busy.

That afternoon, Ms. Patel asked the class how their skills might meet a real need. Liam wrote in his notebook: My skill is remembering names and noticing who is left out. The need is for new students to feel less lost. My idea is a short welcome walk with permission from the office.

The first Friday, Liam nearly hid in the library. He worried that a welcome walk would feel awkward and that the new student might not want a tour. Then he remembered that commitment could mean returning after a wobble. He asked Ms. Patel whether he could join the tour crew with one other student. She suggested they ask first and keep the walk to twenty minutes.

The next week, Liam and Priya welcomed a new student together. They pointed out the music room, the lunchroom recycling bins, and a bench that stayed sunny in the afternoon. Liam was still nervous, but he had a useful role. He learned that community contribution does not require the loudest voice. Greeters, translators, careful listeners, and people who put chairs away all make a school work better. A strength becomes especially helpful when it is matched to a real need.`,activity:`Students complete a match-up table: three skills, three possible contributions, one they could actually try at school this month. If your school has real jobs, post them.`,worksheetTitle:`Skill-to-contribution match`,fields:[{kind:`table`,id:`match`,columns:[`A skill or knowledge I have`,`A community need it could help`,`School or community idea`],rows:3},{kind:`text`,id:`try`,label:`My realistic try this month (one idea, with when and who I would ask)`,rows:2},{kind:`text`,id:`link`,label:`How this links to perseverance (what might be awkward at first)`},{kind:`exit`,id:`exit`,prompt:`Complete: I can contribute by _____ because I _____.`}],sample:{match:`Remembering names and noticing who is left out | New students feeling lost | Friday welcome walk with the office`,try:`Ask Ms. Patel this Friday if I can join the new-kid tour crew, twenty minutes, with one other student.`,link:`The first time will feel awkward. Commitment includes coming back after a wobble.`,exit:`I can contribute by greeting new students because I remember names and I know how a new building feels.`},checks:[`Is the try realistic for Grade 5 (time, permission, local)?`,`Did they connect a real skill, not a superhero identity?`],tip:`If a student cannot volunteer outside school, school jobs still count. Do not grade the size of the project.`,preview:`Next week: protective factors — the supports that help us keep going.`},{week:5,unitId:`character`,title:`Protective Factors That Help Us Keep Going`,focus:`Supports for perseverance`,outcomeFocus:`Protective factors are conditions or attributes in individuals, families, and communities that support well-being and help people persevere.`,materials:[`Week 5 worksheet`,`Board: People · Places · Skills · Routines`],knowledge:[`Protective factors support perseverance and well-being (supportive relationships, problem-solving, sense of purpose, belonging, rest, asking for help).`,`Protective factors can be people, places, skills, or routines.`],understanding:[`Perseverance is more likely when protective factors are in place — it is not only willpower.`],skills:[`Identify protective factors that support perseverance.`],warmUp:`Ask: “When you have a hard week, what actually helps — not what people say should help?” Collect four answers. Sort them into People, Places, Skills, Routines.`,teachTitle:`Willpower is not the whole story`,teachSteps:[`A protective factor is something that makes it more likely you can keep going and stay well: a person who listens, a place that feels safe, a skill you can use, a routine that steadies you.`,`People: a coach, a cousin, a teacher, a neighbour, a classmate who saves you a seat.`,`Places: the library nook, a community league rink, grandma’s kitchen, the art room at lunch.`,`Skills: asking for a break, breaking a task into steps, naming a feeling, using the fingering chart.`,`Routines: sleep, water bottle, practise after snack, Friday check-in with a trusted adult.`,`Protective factors do not erase hard things. They make staying with the hard thing more possible.`],story:`Jordan stared at his math workbook and decided the new unit looked like a wall. The numbers seemed to crowd together, and the first page had already collected three eraser crumbs.

"I am just bad at this," he muttered.

His teacher, Ms. Grant, heard him but did not rush to disagree. She asked Jordan what had helped during the last difficult unit. He made a list. There was Ms. Grant's Tuesday after-school table, where he could ask questions without feeling hurried. There was a strategy of doing the first two questions and then raising his hand. There was his bedtime routine, which meant going to sleep before the late hockey recap.

Jordan noticed that none of those supports was simply "try harder." He had tried working later while tired, and it had not helped. He chose one factor to strengthen: on Thursday, he would ask his uncle Ravi, who liked puzzles, to sit with him for ten minutes. Ravi would not do the questions for him. He would help Jordan explain what the problem was asking.

On Thursday, Jordan brought his workbook to the kitchen table. The first problem still looked difficult. Ravi asked him to underline the information he knew. After ten minutes, Jordan had not finished the whole page, but he had started the first two questions and knew what to ask Ms. Grant the next day.

Jordan returned to class with a different plan. Perseverance was not a test of willpower performed alone. A helpful person, place, skill, or routine could make continuing possible. Protective factors did not erase the wall, but they gave him a way to find a door through it.`,activity:`Students map protective factors they already have and one they could strengthen this month. Private is allowed. Everyday is enough.`,worksheetTitle:`My protective-factor card`,fields:[{kind:`table`,id:`factors`,columns:[`Kind`,`One I already have`,`How it helps me keep going`],rowLabels:[`Person`,`Place`,`Skill`,`Routine`]},{kind:`text`,id:`strengthen`,label:`One protective factor I could strengthen this month (small and realistic)`,rows:2},{kind:`exit`,id:`exit`,prompt:`Why is “just try harder” a weak plan compared with naming a protective factor?`}],sample:{factors:`Person: Ms. Grant’s Tuesday table. Place: library nook. Skill: first two questions then hand up. Routine: sleep before the recap.`,strengthen:`Ask my uncle to sit with me for ten minutes on Thursday while I start the review sheet.`,exit:`Trying harder with no sleep already failed. A person, place, skill, or routine gives me a way through — not just more grinding.`},checks:[`Did they name factors in more than one category?`,`Is the “strengthen” item small enough to actually try?`],tip:`Do not force family details. School adults and school places are valid protective factors. If a student cannot name a person, help them name one at school.`,preview:`Next week we wrap the unit: relating life experiences to well-being.`},{week:6,unitId:`character`,title:`Relating Life Experiences to Well-Being`,focus:`Unit wrap`,outcomeFocus:`Pull the thread: volunteering, experiences, perseverance, contribution, and protective factors all relate to well-being.`,materials:[`Week 6 worksheet`,`Unit rubric (teacher)`],knowledge:[`Well-being includes feeling able, belonging, and having purpose — not only “being happy.”`],understanding:[`Life experiences, perseverance, and contribution can support well-being when protective factors are in place.`],skills:[`Reflect and relate life experiences to perseverance and well-being.`,`Describe how perseverance can affect well-being.`],warmUp:`Board: purpose · belonging · confidence · mood · health. Ask: which of these can volunteering or perseverance touch? Take two examples from earlier weeks (lost-and-found, recorder, welcome walk).`,teachTitle:`Tell the whole story in one page`,teachSteps:[`This unit’s outcome: reflect and relate life experiences to perseverance and well-being.`,`A strong wrap does four jobs: names an experience, names a perseverance move, names a contribution or skill, and names an effect on well-being.`,`Well-being is not a smiley-face contest. “I dread band less” counts. “Younger kids got their hats back” counts.`,`Use the rubric once near the end of the six weeks. Worksheets stay formative.`],story:`On Friday, Ms. Patel placed six familiar objects on a table: a lost-and-found sign, a soccer warm-up card, a recorder fingering chart, a welcome-walk map, a math workbook, and a small blue agenda.

"These are pieces of our unit," she said. "Today, connect one life experience to perseverance and well-being."

Maya remembered staying after school to sort wet mittens. Noah remembered asking for rehearsal time before a quiz. Aisha remembered slowing down the high B and resting her jaw. Liam remembered feeling awkward on the first welcome walk but returning the next Friday.

Jordan picked up the math workbook. At the beginning of the unit, the pages had made him want to close his binder and decide he was bad at math. He had thought perseverance meant forcing himself to work alone until the problem disappeared. The Tuesday help table, his first-two-questions strategy, and a short visit with his uncle had changed that idea.

Jordan opened his notebook and wrote: Because math felt like a wall, I practised perseverance by starting with two questions, using the Tuesday table, and asking for ten minutes of help. That supported my well-being because I stopped calling myself bad at math, felt less stuck, and kept showing up.

He read the sentence to his group. No one applauded, and no one needed to. The words showed a complete connection: an experience had shaped his feelings, a practical support had helped him continue, and continuing had changed how he felt about himself.

Ms. Patel reminded the class that well-being was not the same as feeling cheerful every minute. Sometimes it meant having less worry, more support, or enough confidence to try again. Their stories were ordinary school stories, but they still mattered. Nobody had saved the world. Each person had noticed something difficult, found a way through, and learned what helped them keep going.`,activity:`Students write a short wrap using the four-part frame. Optional share of the last sentence only. Then a quick self-check against the rubric (proficient is the target).`,worksheetTitle:`Relating the pieces`,fields:[{kind:`text`,id:`exp`,label:`One life experience from this unit or from my year (everyday is enough)`},{kind:`text`,id:`pers`,label:`How I practised perseverance (effort, courage, commitment, belief, or a new plan)`},{kind:`text`,id:`give`,label:`A skill I can contribute, or a volunteer idea I still think is realistic`},{kind:`text`,id:`protect`,label:`One protective factor I will keep using`},{kind:`exit`,id:`exit`,prompt:`Because of ____, I practised perseverance by ____, and that helped well-being because ____.`}],sample:{exp:`Math this month felt like a wall.`,pers:`I used the first-two-questions skill and went to the Tuesday table. Rest on Wednesday so I could come back Thursday.`,give:`I can help a younger student who is stuck on a first question, because I know how that freeze feels.`,protect:`Tuesday after-school table and sleep before late recaps.`,exit:`Because math felt like a wall, I practised perseverance by using a skill and a person, and that helped well-being because I stopped calling myself bad at it and I showed up.`},checks:[`Does the exit sentence include experience, perseverance, and well-being?`,`Did they avoid a hero speech?`],tip:`Score the unit rubric once now or next class. Do not score every worksheet out of 4. Proficient is the target.`,preview:`Weeks 7–12: Healthy Relationships — perspectives, listening, communication.`}],t=[{week:7,unitId:`relationships`,title:`How Experiences Shape Perspectives`,focus:`What a perspective is`,outcomeFocus:`A perspective is how someone understands, thinks, and feels about a situation. Experiences, personal factors, and environment can shape it.`,materials:[`Week 7 worksheet`,`Simple scenario cards (on the worksheet)`],knowledge:[`A perspective is how someone understands, thinks, and feels about a situation.`,`Experiences, personal factors, and environment can influence perspective.`],understanding:[`Two people can both be honest and still see a situation differently.`],skills:[`Describe how experiences can affect perspectives of self and others.`],warmUp:`Hold up an object (mug, ball). Students on the left describe only what they see; students on the right do the same. Point out: same object, different view. That is a tiny model of perspective.`,teachTitle:`Perspective is not the same as “right vs wrong”`,teachSteps:[`Perspective = how a person understands a situation from where they stand — their experiences, feelings, and information.`,`Two people can both be honest and still see a recess problem differently.`,`Factors that influence perspective: past experiences, culture and family, how tired or hungry you are, where you were standing, what you heard first.`,`We acknowledge a perspective when we can say it fairly, even if we do not agree.`],story:`Ms. Patel placed a chipped green mug on the centre table. Students sitting near the windows could see its handle and a bright yellow sticker. Students near the door could see a crack running from the rim toward the bottom.

"Which detail is the real mug?" Ms. Patel asked.

Both groups laughed. They knew the answer was both. They were looking from different places.

Then the class considered a soccer scenario. Sam had the ball during a lunchtime game and had not passed it for several minutes. Riley complained that Sam was hogging it, just like a student had done the year before. Sam looked confused. Sam had joined the school only that month and had never heard about the class rotation rule.

Elena wrote Sam's perspective: Sam thinks the game is open, and nobody explained the rotation. Mateo wrote Riley's: last year's unfair game made this situation look like the same pattern. Neither student had to be declared completely right or wrong for the class to understand what was happening.

After lunch, Ms. Patel asked Sam and Riley to explain their views to each other. Riley said, "When one person keeps the ball, I worry nobody else will get a turn." Sam replied, "I thought we were supposed to keep playing until someone asked for a pass." They agreed on a rotation for the rest of the game.

The mug had shown the class that a perspective is the way a person understands a situation from where they stand. Experiences, memories, information, and position can all affect that view. Two people can describe an event honestly and still notice different parts. Acknowledging a perspective does not mean agreeing with every conclusion. It means making an effort to say what the other person sees fairly before deciding what to do next.`,activity:`Students read two short recess scenarios and write each person’s likely perspective. No picking a winner yet — that is a later week.`,worksheetTitle:`Two perspectives`,fields:[{kind:`intro`,text:`Scenario A. Sam used the soccer ball at recess. Riley has been waiting and says Sam always hogged it last year. Sam just joined the school this month and did not know there was a rotation.`},{kind:`table`,id:`a`,columns:[`Person`,`What they might think / feel`,`What experience might sit behind that`],rowLabels:[`Sam`,`Riley`]},{kind:`intro`,text:`Scenario B. The teacher asks the class to move seats. Kai is glad (talking neighbours). Morgan is upset (liked the quiet corner).`},{kind:`table`,id:`b`,columns:[`Person`,`Perspective in one sentence`],rowLabels:[`Kai`,`Morgan`]},{kind:`exit`,id:`exit`,prompt:`Name one personal or environmental factor that can change a perspective.`}],sample:{a:`Sam: This is an open game. Nobody told me a rule. Just joined this month. | Riley: Last year felt hogged, so this year already looks unfair.`,b:`Kai: I am glad because I like talking neighbours. | Morgan: I am upset because the quiet corner helped me work.`,exit:`Where you were standing, what you heard first, or how tired you are — like being stuck as goalie the whole recess.`},checks:[`Can they define perspective without using “they’re wrong”?`,`Did they write both sides fairly in Scenario A?`,`Can they name a factor (experience, place, information)?`],tip:`Stop any “Riley is just dramatic” talk. The skill is fair restating. You can disagree later; you cannot skip understanding.`,preview:`Next week we listen so others feel heard.`},{week:8,unitId:`relationships`,title:`Listening So Others Feel Heard`,focus:`Listening skills`,outcomeFocus:`Listening skills that support understanding another person’s perspective — not listening to reload your own point.`,materials:[`Week 8 worksheet`,`Partner seating`],knowledge:[`Listening skills include paying attention, waiting, paraphrasing, and asking clarifying questions.`],understanding:[`Perspectives can be acknowledged when people listen to understand, not to reload.`],skills:[`Apply listening skills to understand another person's perspective.`],warmUp:`Model two 20-second listens. First: you interrupt, look away, and say “same, but me.” Second: you face the speaker, wait, and paraphrase. Ask which one made the speaker feel heard.`,teachTitle:`Four listening moves`,teachSteps:[`Body: face the person, devices down, don’t fake-listen while scanning the room.`,`Wait: let them finish. Silence of two seconds is allowed.`,`Paraphrase: “So you felt ___ because ___.” Check: “Did I get that right?”`,`Curious question: one question that is not a trap. “What happened next?” beats “Why would you do that?”`],story:`Harper and Dev sat across from each other with a timer between them. Their task was to practise listening without preparing a reply while the other person spoke. Dev chose a problem from art class.

"Our group opened the supply cupboard, and all the good markers were gone," he said. "We had to start late, and I thought Ms. Chen would blame us for not being ready."

Harper's first instinct was to tell Dev about the time her own group had lost the scissors. She held the story in her mind and let Dev finish. Then she faced him, waited two quiet seconds, and tried a paraphrase.

"You felt rushed because the marker bin was empty, and you worried the teacher would think your group had caused the problem?"

Dev nodded, but then shook his head a little. "Partly. I also felt embarrassed because I thought the other groups were watching us."

Harper asked a curious question that was not a trap. "What happened next?"

Dev explained that another student had shared a blue marker, but by then he had missed the instructions for the map labels. Harper summarized again, including the embarrassment and the missed directions. This time, Dev said, "Yes. That is what I meant."

Then they swapped roles. Harper described a time her stomach dropped before reading aloud. Dev wanted to say, "That happened to me too," but he waited. He faced her, paraphrased her point, and asked one question. Harper marked the worksheet: You heard me.

The hardest move was waiting. Silence felt like failing, but it gave each speaker room to finish. The class wrote four listening moves on the board: face the speaker, wait, say their view in your own words, and ask a curious question. Listening was not agreeing or fixing the problem. It was showing another person that their experience had reached you.`,activity:`Pairs: Speaker has 45 seconds on “a time something felt unfair at school (no names).” Listener uses the four moves, then writes the paraphrase. Swap. If odd number, a triad with an observer.`,worksheetTitle:`Listen and paraphrase`,fields:[{kind:`intro`,text:`Speaker topic: a school-appropriate time something felt unfair (no classmate names).`},{kind:`text`,id:`para`,label:`Their perspective in my words`,rows:3},{kind:`text`,id:`q`,label:`The question I asked`},{kind:`choice`,id:`interrupt`,label:`Did I interrupt?`,options:[`Yes`,`No`]},{kind:`choice`,id:`look`,label:`Did I look at them?`,options:[`Yes`,`No`]},{kind:`choice`,id:`heard`,label:`Partner’s check: “You heard me.”`,options:[`Yes`,`Partly`,`Not yet`]},{kind:`exit`,id:`exit`,prompt:`Which listening move is hardest for you, and why?`}],sample:{para:`So you felt rushed because the art bin was empty and you thought our group would get blamed for not starting. Did I get that right?`,q:`What happened next? (not a trap question)`,interrupt:`No`,look:`Yes`,heard:`Partly`,exit:`Waiting. Silence feels like I am failing, but it is actually the work so I do not reload my own story.`},checks:[`Did the paraphrase match what the speaker meant?`,`Was there one curious question that was not an attack?`,`Can they name a listening move they still need?`],tip:`Sit where you can hear interruptions. Praise the paraphrase, not the drama of the story. Coaching sentence: “Say their view, not your verdict.”`,preview:`Next week: communicating with respect — fact, effect, ask.`},{week:9,unitId:`relationships`,title:`Communicating With Respect`,focus:`Clear, kind, specific`,outcomeFocus:`Respectful communication skills — I-messages, specific requests, tone and body language that match the words.`,materials:[`Week 9 worksheet`],knowledge:[`Communication skills include using respectful language, tone, and body language; being specific; using I-messages.`],understanding:[`A specific ask works better than a put-down because the other person can actually do the thing.`],skills:[`Apply communication skills to connect perspectives respectfully.`],warmUp:`Read two lines in a flat voice then a sharp voice: “I wasn’t finished with that.” Same words, different impact. Students vote: which one invites a conversation?`,teachTitle:`A respectful message has three parts`,teachSteps:[`What I noticed (fact): “The markers were dumped in the bin.”`,`How it affected me (feeling or need): “I couldn’t find a working one.”`,`A clear ask: “Please put lids on and stand them up.”`,`Avoid you-always attacks, sarcasm, and public call-outs. Respect includes volume, timing (not in front of a crowd if it can wait), and accepting that the other person may need a minute.`],story:`After math, Harper noticed that several markers had been dumped into the supply bin without their lids. The next group would need them for map labels, and dry markers were not much help.

Dev saw the same mess. In his head, a sentence appeared: You always wreck our group. He knew that sentence might feel satisfying for one second, but it would probably make the other person argue about the word always instead of helping with the markers.

Ms. Chen asked the class to turn the complaint into a respectful message with three parts: say what you noticed, explain how it affected you, and make a clear request. Dev wrote, I noticed the markers were put away without their lids. It affected me because I could not find a working marker for the map labels. Could you put the lids on and stand them up in the bin?

He read it once in a flat voice and once in a sharp voice. The words were the same, but the sharp version sounded like an attack. Harper suggested taking a breath before the request. Dev tried again, speaking clearly without pretending he was delighted about the mess.

He found the student who had used the markers. "I noticed the lids were left off," Dev said. "I could not find a working one for our labels. Could you help me put the lids on?"

The student looked surprised, then admitted he had been hurrying to lunch. Together, they capped the markers and returned them to the bin.

Dev crossed out thief, stupid, and stop being awful from his draft. Those words described a person or an insult; they did not explain what needed to happen. A respectful message could still be honest about a problem. When it was clear, kind, and specific, the other person had a fair chance to understand and do something useful.

The next group found a working marker for every map label. That small result felt better than winning an argument.`,activity:`Rewrite three blunt lines into respectful three-part messages. Then write one real line they might need this week (optional to use in real life).`,worksheetTitle:`Rewrite for respect`,fields:[{kind:`intro`,text:`Change each blunt line into: I noticed… It affected me… Could you…`},{kind:`table`,id:`rewrite`,columns:[`Blunt line`,`Respectful three-part message`],rowLabels:[`You always wreck our group.`,`Give it back, thief.`,`That’s a stupid idea.`]},{kind:`text`,id:`real`,label:`One real situation I might need this for (no names required)`,rows:2},{kind:`exit`,id:`exit`,prompt:`Why does a specific ask work better than a put-down?`}],sample:{rewrite:`I noticed the markers were dumped without lids. It affected me because I could not find a working one. Could you put lids on and stand them up? | I noticed my eraser did not come back after math. Could you return it at recess? | I noticed a different plan. Could we try yours for five minutes and then check?`,real:`A borrowed eraser that never came back. Ask at recess, not during the mini-lesson.`,exit:`The other person can actually do the thing. Stop being awful is not a job. Please return the eraser after math is.`},checks:[`Do the rewrites include a fact, an effect, and an ask?`,`Did they drop sarcasm?`,`Can they explain why timing and tone matter?`],tip:`Collect one strong rewrite under the document camera with no student names on it.`,preview:`Next week: when perspectives differ — shared understanding, not a winner.`},{week:10,unitId:`relationships`,title:`When Perspectives Differ`,focus:`Shared understanding`,outcomeFocus:`Shared understanding is not the same as full agreement. It means both people can say the other view fairly and name a next step.`,materials:[`Week 10 worksheet`],knowledge:[`Shared understanding includes restating both sides, naming overlap, and agreeing on a next step even if the whole story is not settled.`],understanding:[`Healthy relationships are supported when perspectives are considered, even when people still disagree.`],skills:[`Work cooperatively to reach a shared understanding.`],warmUp:`Return to Sam and Riley’s soccer ball. Ask: if both perspectives are honest, what is a next step that does not pick a villain?`,teachTitle:`Three moves toward shared understanding`,teachSteps:[`Restate both views fairly (Week 7 skill).`,`Name overlap: both want a fair game; both want to play.`,`Agree on a next step: a rotation, a timer, a third person as referee for one recess — not a courtroom.`,`You can still disagree about last year. You do not have to solve the whole history in 12 minutes.`],story:`On Tuesday afternoon, Ms. Chen put a tub of beanbags beside the gym door. The class was supposed to choose a game for indoor recess. Riley immediately suggested the three-touch passing game their class had enjoyed last year. Sam folded his arms. He remembered that the game had ended with arguments and one student being left out.

Riley heard Sam say, "That game was a disaster," and felt a familiar tightness in his chest. Sam heard Riley say, "You always ruin the fun," and felt accused. Their voices began to climb, so Ms. Chen asked them to pause. No one had to decide who was the winner of the argument.

First, Riley explained what he remembered. He liked that everyone got to move and that teams could change quickly. Sam repeated Riley's idea in his own words, and Riley nodded. Then Sam explained his memory: the rules had never been clear, and some students had barely touched the ball. Riley repeated that view without adding, "but."

On the whiteboard, they wrote the overlap: both students wanted a fair game, clear rules, and a chance for everyone to play. Their next step was a three-touch rotation. After every three passes, the ball would go to a new player, and the class would check in on Friday.

The game was not perfect, but fewer students were waiting on the edges. Riley and Sam grinned when the final pass reached a classmate who had been quiet. Shared understanding did not mean Riley got his way or Sam won. It meant each could describe the other view fairly, then help choose a plan they could both live with.`,activity:`Students take one scenario (soccer, seats, or a group-project idea clash) and complete: both views, overlap, next step.`,worksheetTitle:`Shared understanding, not a winner`,fields:[{kind:`text`,id:`scene`,label:`The situation (no real classmate names)`},{kind:`text`,id:`view1`,label:`Person A’s view, said fairly`},{kind:`text`,id:`view2`,label:`Person B’s view, said fairly`},{kind:`text`,id:`overlap`,label:`Overlap (what both people want or need)`},{kind:`text`,id:`next`,label:`A next step both could live with`},{kind:`exit`,id:`exit`,prompt:`How is shared understanding different from winning an argument?`}],sample:{scene:`Soccer ball at recess — rotation not explained to a new student.`,view1:`Sam thinks it is an open game and feels accused.`,view2:`Riley remembers last year and already feels left out.`,overlap:`Both want a fair game and both want to play.`,next:`Three-touch rotation for the rest of the week, posted on the door, check-in Friday.`,exit:`Each person can say the other view without twisting it, and both can live with the next step. Nobody has to be the villain.`},checks:[`Are both views fair, not sarcastic?`,`Is the next step doable this week?`],tip:`If a real recess conflict is live, do not put those two students on the spot. Use the worksheet scenario.`,preview:`Next week we practise the skills on a shared task.`},{week:11,unitId:`relationships`,title:`Working Together on a Shared Task`,focus:`Practise the skills`,outcomeFocus:`Apply listening, respectful talk, and shared understanding while actually working together.`,materials:[`Week 11 worksheet`,`One shared task (booth plan, classroom job roster, or recess-game rules)`],knowledge:[`Respectful collaboration includes listening, specific communication, and sharing the work.`],understanding:[`Skills only count if they show up when the work is real.`],skills:[`Demonstrate respectful communication while working on a shared task.`],warmUp:`Pairs have 60 seconds to agree on a team name for a pretend class fair booth. Then: who talked more? Who asked a question?`,teachTitle:`The task is the practice`,teachSteps:[`Today’s job is small and real: plan a class fair booth in 12 minutes (name, two jobs, one material list).`,`Use the four listening moves and the three-part message if you get stuck.`,`Shared understanding: if you disagree, restate, find overlap, pick a next step.`,`Everyone writes. Nobody is only the boss or only the scribe unless you rotate.`],story:`The spring fair was six days away, and Ms. Okafor's Grade 5 class had one table to plan. Harper wanted a popcorn booth. She imagined the buttery smell bringing people over from the gym. Dev wanted a bookmark and sticker stall because it would be easier for students to run. Harper started to say, "Bookmarks are boring," while Dev prepared to answer, "Popcorn is too much work."

Instead, Harper took a breath. "So you are worried about the hot plate, supervision, and keeping everyone safe?" she asked. Dev looked surprised, then nodded. "And you want something people will actually visit, not just a table of worksheets." Harper nodded too. The disagreement had been hiding two reasonable concerns.

They listed what they both wanted: a booth Grade 5 students could manage, a design younger children would enjoy, and enough money for the class field trip. Dev suggested bookmarks shaped like popcorn bags. Harper sketched a yellow-and-red sticker that said, "Read, Then Pass It On." They decided they would not need a hot plate at all.

Harper gathered card stock, markers, and safe stickers. Dev made a large sign and checked the price list. They asked two classmates to test whether the bookmarks fit inside library books. When the first sign fell off the table, neither blamed the other. Dev found stronger tape, and Harper moved the sign where people could see it.

At the fair, children lined up to choose a bookmark and decorate a sticker. The booth was simple, but it worked because the plan belonged to both partners. Harper learned that listening could improve an idea without erasing it. Dev learned that a shared task was not about proving whose first suggestion was best. The task was the practice: listen, find the overlap, divide the work, and keep adjusting together.`,activity:`Pairs or triads complete a booth (or classroom job) plan. Each person fills the process side of the worksheet: one listen, one respectful line, one compromise.`,worksheetTitle:`Shared task — class fair booth`,fields:[{kind:`text`,id:`booth`,label:`Booth name and what we will offer`},{kind:`table`,id:`jobs`,columns:[`Job`,`Who`],rows:3},{kind:`text`,id:`listen`,label:`One time I paraphrased or asked a curious question`},{kind:`text`,id:`talk`,label:`One respectful three-part line we needed (or could have used)`},{kind:`text`,id:`share`,label:`Where our perspectives differed and what we did`},{kind:`exit`,id:`exit`,prompt:`Which relationship skill showed up in the work, not just on the page?`}],sample:{booth:`Bookmark-and-sticker stall with a popcorn-themed design. No hot plate.`,jobs:`Materials — Harper | Sign — Dev | Both names on the plan`,listen:`So you are worried about safety and an adult, not about bookmarks being cooler.`,talk:`I noticed popcorn needs a hot plate. It affects whether a supervisor has to stand there. Could we keep the theme without cooking?`,share:`Popcorn vs bookmarks. Overlap: a booth kids visit that Grade 5 can run. Next step: theme without the hot plate.`,exit:`Paraphrase. We almost said fine, whatever. Restating the worry saved the plan.`},checks:[`Did more than one person have a real job?`,`Is there evidence of listening or a respectful ask, not only a pretty booth name?`],tip:`If a pair finishes in four minutes, they are skipping the process. Sit beside them and ask for the paraphrase out loud.`,preview:`Next week: unit wrap — connecting perspectives to healthy relationships.`},{week:12,unitId:`relationships`,title:`Connecting Perspectives to Healthy Relationships`,focus:`Unit wrap`,outcomeFocus:`Healthy relationships are supported when we acknowledge perspectives, listen, communicate with respect, and work toward shared understanding.`,materials:[`Week 12 worksheet`,`Unit rubric`],knowledge:[`Healthy relationships are built on communication, collaboration, empathy, and respect.`],understanding:[`Perspectives of self and others can be acknowledged and connected through communication and listening.`],skills:[`Acknowledge and connect perspectives of self and others through communication and listening skills.`],warmUp:`Four corners of the board: perspective, listen, respectful talk, shared understanding. Students put one sticky (or one spoken line) on the skill they used most this unit and one they still need.`,teachTitle:`Name the outcome in kid language`,teachSteps:[`The outcome: acknowledge and connect perspectives of self and others through communication and listening skills.`,`Acknowledge = say it fairly. Connect = find overlap and a next step. Communication and listening = the tools.`,`A healthy relationship is not “we never disagree.” It is “we can disagree without erasing each other.”`,`Score the unit rubric once. Proficient is the target.`],story:`By the end of the unit, Elena had started noticing how many small problems happened in one school day. At recess, two students reached for the same soccer ball. In art, a classmate thought someone had taken the blue paint on purpose. At lunch, Elena's borrowed eraser came back with a corner missing. None of these moments needed a villain, but each one could make a relationship feel wobbly.

During a class circle, Ms. Bouchard asked students to choose one ordinary disagreement and practise a better next step. Elena partnered with Noor. Elena pretended the soccer ball was hers because she had brought it outside first. Noor pretended she had been waiting for a turn.

Elena began, "You think you should get the ball because you have not had a turn yet, and you want the game to be fair." Noor said that was her view. Then Noor said, "You think bringing the ball out means you should start with it, and you want the game to keep moving." Elena agreed.

They waited two quiet seconds instead of rushing to defend themselves. Their plan was specific: Elena would take the first shot, Noor would take the next, and they would invite another player to keep score. It was not a grand solution. It was clear enough to try.

Elena wrote the class learning in kid language on a sticky note: "I can say someone else's view without twisting it. I can say mine without attacking. Then we can choose a next step." She added a small heart beside the words, not because every disagreement felt nice, but because respectful problem-solving helped people feel safe and valued.

On Friday, Elena used the same moves when a friend was upset about the missing eraser corner. The friendship did not depend on never disagreeing. It grew when both people could be heard and could help repair what happened. That was how connecting perspectives became part of a healthy relationship.`,activity:`Students complete a four-skill wrap and one “I still need” goal. Optional: one sentence they are willing to use this week at recess.`,worksheetTitle:`Relationship wrap`,fields:[{kind:`table`,id:`skills`,columns:[`Skill`,`One example from this unit (or from real life, no names)`],rowLabels:[`Perspective (say it fairly)`,`Listening`,`Respectful talk`,`Shared understanding`]},{kind:`text`,id:`need`,label:`The skill I still need, and one way I will practise it`},{kind:`exit`,id:`exit`,prompt:`In kid language: what does it mean to connect perspectives?`}],sample:{skills:`Sam/Riley soccer — both honest. | Paraphrase of the empty art bin. | I-message about lids. | Three-touch rotation as a next step.`,need:`Waiting. I will count two seconds before I jump in with my story.`,exit:`I can say someone else’s view without twisting it, say mine without attacking, and pick a next step together.`},checks:[`Can they state the outcome without the word “winning”?`,`Did they name a skill they still need?`],tip:`Use the rubric now. Do not average six worksheets. Look at the wrap plus one strong earlier sheet.`,preview:`Weeks 13–18: Safety — responsibility, privacy, consent and refusal.`}],n=[{week:13,unitId:`safety`,title:`Responsibility and the Impact of Choices`,focus:`Actions have consequences`,outcomeFocus:`Responsibility means owning a choice, naming who it affects, and repairing if needed.`,materials:[`Week 13 worksheet`,`Timer`],knowledge:[`Responsibility includes accepting the results of one's actions.`,`Impacts can be physical, social-emotional, or practical (time, money, trust).`],understanding:[`Actions have consequences that can affect the well-being of self and others.`],skills:[`Analyze the impact of actions on the well-being of self and others.`],warmUp:`Chain: a backpack in the aisle. Who besides the owner is affected? List three impacts.`,teachTitle:`Responsibility is not the same as blame`,teachSteps:[`Responsible = I own my choice, I can name who it affects, I can repair.`,`Impacts can be physical, social-emotional, or practical (time, money, trust).`,`Factors: hurry, audience, not knowing the rule, mood, trying to be funny.`,`An apology without a changed next choice is incomplete.`],story:`Owen was hurrying to get to music when he dropped his backpack beside the classroom door. He noticed it was in the aisle, but he thought, "I'll move it in a second." Then he ran to join the line. A moment later, Ms. Patel stepped around the bag and nearly tripped. The class lost time while everyone stopped. Owen's water bottle rolled underneath a desk, opened, and leaked across Maya's duo-tang.

Owen felt his face grow hot. He wanted to say the bag had only been there for a moment, or that the bottle had opened by accident. Ms. Patel reminded him that responsibility was not the same as building a shame pile. It meant noticing the choice, naming its impact, and helping repair the result.

Owen began with the physical impact: Ms. Patel could have fallen. Then he named the practical impact: the class lost a minute, and Maya's work was wet. Finally, he considered the social impact. Maya might feel that her work had not been treated carefully.

He picked up the backpack, dried the floor, and asked Maya which pages were damaged. At lunch, he offered to recopy them. Maya still felt disappointed, but she appreciated that Owen had not pretended nothing happened.

Later, the class discussed a made-up example about forwarding a private message. They traced what could be affected: trust, feelings, and a person's sense of control. Repair would include deleting the message, apologizing to the person privately, telling a trusted adult if it had spread, and making a different choice next time.

Before leaving for music, Owen moved his backpack fully under his desk. The choice was small, but it showed the lesson. Being responsible did not mean being a bad person or never making mistakes. It meant understanding that choices travel outward, then taking honest action to make things safer and better.`,activity:`Trace three scenarios: choice → who is affected → responsible next step. No real class incidents as examples.`,worksheetTitle:`Choice → impact → repair`,fields:[{kind:`table`,id:`trace`,columns:[`Situation`,`Who could be affected?`,`Responsible next step`],rowLabels:[`You forward a screenshot of a classmate's private message.`,`You sit at the nut-aware table with a granola bar.`,`You said you would walk a younger buddy home, then went to the park.`]},{kind:`text`,id:`real`,label:`One real choice I made this month (small is fine) and who it affected`,rows:2},{kind:`exit`,id:`exit`,prompt:`Responsibility includes accepting results. What does that mean in your own words?`}],sample:{trace:`Classmate (trust), anyone who sees it, you — delete, apologize, do not repeat. | Allergic classmate, supervisors — move, tell an adult, wash hands. | Buddy, family, teacher — show up or get an adult to cover; apologize.`,real:`I left my backpack in the aisle. Ms. Patel nearly tripped, the class lost a minute, a duo-tang got wet. I picked up the bag, wiped the floor, and recopied the page.`,exit:`I own the choice, I name who it affected, and I repair. An apology without a changed next choice is incomplete.`},checks:[`Named an impact on someone else, not only “I'd get in trouble”?`,`Included repair, not only the word sorry?`],tip:`Do not use a real class incident. Hypotheticals keep dignity intact.`,preview:`Next week: laws, rules, practices, and protocols — why the structures exist.`},{week:14,unitId:`safety`,title:`Laws, Rules, Practices, and Protocols`,focus:`Why the structures exist`,outcomeFocus:`Identify laws, rules, practices, and protocols that support safety and well-being.`,materials:[`Week 14 worksheet`],knowledge:[`Laws, rules, practices, and protocols support safety and well-being.`,`A protocol is a sequence of steps for a specific situation. A rule is a boundary.`],understanding:[`These structures protect people. They work only if people follow them.`],skills:[`Identify laws, rules, practices, and protocols that support safety.`],warmUp:`Students guess the difference between a law, a school rule, a practice, and a protocol before you teach it.`,teachTitle:`Four layers`,teachSteps:[`Law: whole country/province (seatbelts; it is against the law to threaten someone).`,`School rule: our building (phones away, hall passes).`,`Practice: a habit when no one is watching (walk on the right, wash hands).`,`Protocol: agreed steps for a specific situation (fire drill: line, silent, attendance clipboard).`,`If a rule is confusing, ask — do not skip it.`],story:`Fatima liked sorting things into neat categories, so she was confident when Mr. Lewis asked what a protocol was. "It means being polite and following the usual way," she said. "Close," he replied. "Today we will look at four layers and why each one exists."

The class began with a law, such as wearing a seatbelt in a moving car. A law is made for a whole community. Next came a school rule, such as keeping phones in lockers during the day. Then they named a practice: walking on the right side of a hallway so people can pass safely, even when nobody is watching. Finally, they learned that a protocol is an agreed sequence of steps for a particular situation.

Mr. Lewis asked everyone to imagine a fire drill. The class would leave quietly, follow the route, stand with the group, and wait while the teacher checked the attendance clipboard. They discussed who those steps protected. The answer included every person in the building, including visitors and a student who might be in the bathroom. If one part was skipped, the attendance count could be wrong.

The class also reviewed the school's emergency lockdown protocol calmly, without pretending it was a game. If directed by school staff, students would follow the official steps: move out of sight, stay quiet, and wait for instructions or the all-clear. Fatima noticed that the sequence gave people something clear to do when their brains might feel worried.

On the way to lunch, Fatima saw a younger student running in the hallway. She did not lecture him or make up a punishment. She walked on the right and reminded him to slow down near the corner. A rule set a boundary. A practice guided everyday actions. A protocol told people what to do in order. Laws, rules, practices, and protocols were different, but each helped protect people and make shared spaces work.`,activity:`Classify examples, then write one school protocol in order.`,worksheetTitle:`Law, rule, practice, protocol`,fields:[{kind:`intro`,text:`Label: L = law, R = school rule, P = practice, O = protocol.`},{kind:`table`,id:`class`,columns:[`Example`,`L / R / P / O`,`Who does it protect?`],rowLabels:[`Fire drill: silent line, attendance, wait for all-clear`,`Wear a seatbelt`,`Phones in lockers during class`,`Wash hands before eating`,`Lockdown: lights off, out of sight, silent, wait for officials`]},{kind:`text`,id:`steps`,label:`One protocol we use — steps in order`,rows:3},{kind:`exit`,id:`exit`,prompt:`What goes wrong if only some people follow a protocol?`}],sample:{class:`O — everyone in the building. | L — drivers and passengers. | R — learning time and privacy. | P — you and people who share tables. | O — everyone until officials say it is clear.`,steps:`Fire drill: silent line, teacher takes the attendance clipboard, walk to the meeting spot, wait for all-clear, then return.`,exit:`The attendance count is wrong and someone might be left in a hallway. A protocol only works if the steps happen in order for everyone.`},checks:[`Can they tell a protocol (steps) from a general rule?`,`Did they name who is protected?`],tip:`Stay calm on lockdown. This is not a fear lesson. Anxious students: follow up privately.`,preview:`Next week: personal safety and trusted adults.`},{week:15,unitId:`safety`,title:`Personal Safety and Trusted Adults`,focus:`Safe, unsafe, uncomfortable`,outcomeFocus:`Personal safety includes freedom from physical harm, illness, and emotional harm. Name trusted adults.`,materials:[`Week 15 worksheet`,`Question box`],knowledge:[`Personal safety includes freedom from physical harm, illness, and emotional harm.`,`A trusted adult listens, does not ask you to keep unsafe secrets, and can get help.`],understanding:[`Telling to keep someone safe is responsible, not tattling.`],skills:[`Demonstrate responsibility for personal safety.`],warmUp:`Sort: Safe / Uncomfortable / Unsafe. You sort “an adult asking you to keep a special secret about touching” as unsafe.`,teachTitle:`Personal safety plan`,teachSteps:[`Safe: you can learn, play, and rest without harm.`,`Uncomfortable: something feels off. You can say no, leave, and tell an adult even if you are not sure.`,`Unsafe: harm or a body/privacy boundary being broken. Tell a trusted adult the same day. You will not be in trouble for telling.`,`Trusted adult: listens, does not ask you to keep unsafe secrets, can get help. Name more than one, including a school adult.`,`If someone is hurt now: get an adult. Do not try to be the physical hero.`],story:`During health class, Mr. Singh placed three signs on the wall: Safe, Uncomfortable, and Unsafe. He explained that a feeling could be a useful signal, and students could always ask for help. They did not have to solve a confusing situation alone.

The first card described a friend asking to borrow a pencil. Safe, the class agreed. The next described a joke that kept going after someone said it was not funny. That could feel uncomfortable, so the person could say, "Stop," move away, and tell an adult. The last card described an adult asking a child to keep a secret about touching. The class placed it under Unsafe. Mr. Singh was clear: tell a trusted adult the same day, and the child would not be in trouble for telling.

Cole made a personal safety plan in his notebook. He listed his classroom teacher, the office staff member who was there before school, and his aunt, who picked him up on Thursdays. He checked that at least one trusted adult was at school. He also wrote, "If the first person cannot help, I will tell another person and keep telling until someone listens."

Then Mr. Singh described a dare to hide in a bathroom. Cole was not sure whether it was unsafe or simply strange. That uncertainty did not matter. He could say no, leave, and tell an adult. If someone was being hurt right then, he should get an adult quickly instead of trying to be a physical hero. Telling to keep someone safe was responsible, not tattling.

After class, Cole noticed a younger student looking upset near the stairs. He asked if she wanted help finding an adult, then walked with her to the office. Cole did not need to know every detail. His safety plan gave him a next step: notice the feeling, get away from danger, and tell a trusted adult.`,activity:`Personal safety card. Private. Circulate quietly.`,worksheetTitle:`Personal safety card`,fields:[{kind:`table`,id:`adults`,columns:[`Name / role`,`When I could find them`],rows:3},{kind:`text`,id:`uncom`,label:`An uncomfortable (not unsafe) situation`},{kind:`text`,id:`unsure`,label:`If I am not sure it is unsafe, I will`},{kind:`exit`,id:`exit`,prompt:`Why is “keep this special secret” a warning sign when a body or safety boundary is involved?`}],sample:{adults:`Classroom teacher — every school day. | Office adult — before school, recess, lunch. | Aunt on Thursdays — after school, or by phone with a parent.`,uncom:`A joke that goes too far, or a dare to hide in the bathroom. It feels off, but I am not sure it is harm.`,unsure:`Say no, leave, and tell an adult even if I am not sure.`,exit:`Unsafe secrets about bodies or touching are a tell-an-adult-the-same-day situation. A trusted adult does not ask you to keep those.`},checks:[`At least one school adult named?`,`Uncomfortable vs unsafe distinguished?`],tip:`If a student discloses a safety concern: stop, thank them, follow EIPS reporting. Do not promise secrecy. Do not investigate. Inform the principal.`,preview:`Next week: group safety and responsible leadership.`},{week:16,unitId:`safety`,title:`Group Safety and Responsible Leadership`,focus:`Looking after more than yourself`,outcomeFocus:`Group safety includes looking after others, following protocols, and using leadership as responsibility — not as power.`,materials:[`Week 16 worksheet`],knowledge:[`Group safety includes looking after others in a group and following protocols.`,`Leadership in safety is noticing, reminding, and getting an adult — not bossing.`],understanding:[`A group's well-being depends on people taking responsibility for more than themselves.`],skills:[`Demonstrate responsibility for group safety.`],warmUp:`Field-trip picture: one student lags to tie a shoe. Who is responsible besides that student? List three roles.`,teachTitle:`Leadership is a safety job`,teachSteps:[`In a group, your choices affect people who did not make the choice.`,`Responsible leadership: notice, remind kindly, get an adult if the reminder is not enough.`,`Not responsible: mocking, leaving someone, making a dangerous dare to look brave.`,`Examples: bus partner missing, someone not wearing a helmet on a class ride, a group chat piling on.`],story:`On Thursday evening, the community league invited several classes to skate at the outdoor rink. Two Grade 5 students, Amina and Lucas, were chosen as captains for the walk from school. Lucas was excited about being first through the rink gate. He marched ahead, calling, "Keep up!"

At the door, Amina noticed two things. The strap on Jonah's helmet was hanging loose, and Priya, a Grade 3 student, was still taking off her boots in the crowded entryway. Lucas had already started toward the ice. Amina had to choose between following him and checking on the group.

She waited beside Jonah and said, "Let's make sure your helmet is snug before you skate." Jonah adjusted the strap and asked the supervisor to check it. Then Amina looked toward the boot room. Priya was trying to carry her skates and mitts at once. Amina told the supervisor that Priya was still inside, and she stayed where the younger student could see her.

Lucas came back, a little embarrassed. "I thought a captain was supposed to be out front," he said. Amina replied, "Maybe a captain is supposed to know where everyone is." Lucas considered that. He helped Priya carry one skate and counted the students before they entered the rink.

The group moved more slowly than Lucas had planned, but everyone arrived together. The supervisor thanked the captains for paying attention instead of rushing. On the ice, Lucas realized that being first would not have helped anyone if a classmate had been left behind or if a helmet was unsafe.

The next day, the class wrote a sentence about leadership: looking after more than yourself is the job. Power is not the job. A responsible leader notices, waits, reminds respectfully, asks for adult help, and makes sure the group can move safely together.`,activity:`Three group scenarios. For each: who is affected, a responsible leader move, a move that makes it worse.`,worksheetTitle:`Looking after the group`,fields:[{kind:`table`,id:`group`,columns:[`Situation`,`Who is affected?`,`Responsible leader move`],rowLabels:[`Your bus partner is not on the bus and the driver is closing the door.`,`A group chat is forwarding a photo of a classmate who did not agree.`,`Someone dares the group to sneak past the nut-aware table with snacks.`]},{kind:`text`,id:`worse`,label:`One move that would make any of these worse (and why you will not do it)`},{kind:`exit`,id:`exit`,prompt:`How is safety leadership different from being the boss?`}],sample:{group:`Partner, driver, families — tell the driver and a supervisor now. | The classmate, anyone who sees it, you — stop the share, tell an adult. | Allergic classmate, whole table — refuse the dare, move the snacks, tell a supervisor.`,worse:`Mocking, leaving someone, or joining the dare to look brave. That puts the group at risk to protect my image.`,exit:`A leader notices, reminds, and gets an adult. A boss just wants to be first or in charge.`},checks:[`Did they include getting an adult, not only peer heroics?`,`Did they reject the dare/mock path?`],tip:`Praise the quiet notice. The student who waits at the door is leading.`,preview:`Next week: digital privacy and online responsibility.`},{week:17,unitId:`safety`,title:`Digital Privacy and Online Responsibility`,focus:`Digital citizenship`,outcomeFocus:`Digital privacy includes protecting personal information, images, and messages. Digital choices have real impacts.`,materials:[`Week 17 worksheet`],knowledge:[`Digital privacy includes protecting personal information, images, location, passwords, and messages.`,`Once something is forwarded, you do not control it.`],understanding:[`Digital choices have real impacts on privacy and well-being.`],skills:[`Apply practices that protect digital privacy.`],warmUp:`Sort: share freely / ask first / never post. Start with a photo of a classmate, a home address, a lunch selfie of only you, a password hint.`,teachTitle:`Privacy is a well-being issue`,teachSteps:[`Personal information: full name + school + location together is identifying. Photos of other people need permission.`,`Messages and screenshots: private means private. Forwarding is a choice with impact (Week 13).`,`Location tags, school uniforms in photos, and “guess where I am” games can show strangers where you are.`,`Passwords and codes stay off shared docs and off the bottom of laptops.`,`If something spreads: tell a trusted adult. Do not pile on. Do not investigate by sharing it further.`],story:`After school, a group chat buzzed while Sora was finishing her math. Someone had posted a screenshot of a private joke between two classmates. At first, people added laughing emojis. By lunchtime the next day, the screenshot had travelled to students who were not in the chat, and the joke no longer felt funny to the person in it.

Sora had forwarded it once, thinking that passing along a picture was different from saying the words aloud. When she saw the classmate sitting alone, she understood that a tap could travel much farther than a hallway. The impact was not only embarrassment. Trust had been damaged, and the classmate had lost control of a private conversation.

Sora deleted the screenshot from her phone and the chat. She apologized privately without saying, "I was only joking." Then she told the teacher what had happened and asked what else she could do. The teacher explained that if private information spreads, an adult can help make a plan. Sora also decided not to forward anything like that again, even if friends pressured her.

In class, students sorted examples into three groups. They could share a drawing they made or a score from a school game that showed no other faces. They should ask first before sharing a photo with a classmate or a story that named someone. They should never share passwords, addresses, body pictures, or secrets someone had trusted them with.

Sora added one question to her own checklist: "Could this make someone feel unsafe, exposed, or unable to choose?" If the answer might be yes, she would pause and ask. Digital privacy was not just about protecting a device. It was responsibility for her well-being and other people's well-being, especially in a place where one quick tap could reach a crowd.`,activity:`Sort items, then write one personal digital rule they can actually keep.`,worksheetTitle:`Share / ask / never`,fields:[{kind:`table`,id:`sort`,columns:[`Item`,`Share / Ask first / Never`,`Why`],rowLabels:[`Photo of a classmate at recess`,`Your school and the time you walk home`,`A drawing you made`,`A screenshot of a private message`,`A password written under your keyboard`]},{kind:`text`,id:`rule`,label:`One digital rule I can keep this month`},{kind:`exit`,id:`exit`,prompt:`Why does “I was just forwarding it” not cancel the impact?`}],sample:{sort:`Ask first — their face, their permission. | Never — identifies when and where you are alone. | Share — it is yours. | Never / ask an adult — private means private. | Never — anyone at the desk can use it.`,rule:`I will not forward a screenshot of a private message. If I am not sure, I ask the person or an adult.`,exit:`Once it is forwarded, I do not control it. Trust and well-being are already affected. Responsibility includes not doing that.`},checks:[`Ask-first vs never distinguished?`,`Did they name an impact on someone else?`],tip:`Do not ask students to confess what they have already posted. Hypotheticals. If a live incident exists, follow school procedure privately.`,preview:`Next week: consent and refusal — respecting yes and no.`},{week:18,unitId:`safety`,title:`Consent and Refusal — Respecting Yes and No`,focus:`Permission skills`,outcomeFocus:`Consent is permission for something to happen. Refusal is the right to say no. Responsibility includes respecting yes and no. Grade 5: space, photos, games, borrowing, digital sharing — not sexual activity.`,materials:[`Week 18 worksheet`,`Question box`],knowledge:[`Consent is permission for something to happen or agreement to do something.`,`Refusal is the right to say no. Silence or freeze is not a yes.`,`Responsibility includes respecting the acceptance or refusal of consent from another.`],understanding:[`Respecting yes and no protects well-being and trust.`],skills:[`Demonstrate Grade 5-appropriate consent and refusal (space, photos, games, borrowing, digital sharing).`],warmUp:`Four quick scenes: hug, photo, borrowing a hoodie, tagging someone in a post. Students show yes / no / ask first with fingers. No discussion of bodies as a joke.`,teachTitle:`Ask, wait, respect`,teachSteps:[`Ask first for space (hugs, crowding), photos, games that involve touching, borrowing, and posting someone’s image or words.`,`Wait for a clear yes. Silence, maybe, or freeze is not a yes. “I guess” under pressure is not a yes.`,`No can be said with words, a shake of the head, walking away, or “stop.” All of those count.`,`If you hear no: stop. Do not negotiate, tickle harder, or “it was just a joke.”`,`You can refuse. You do not need a long reason. A trusted adult can help if someone will not stop.`,`This lesson is school-appropriate: permission for space, photos, games, borrowing, and digital sharing. Not sexual activity.`],story:`On the last day before the class picnic, Priya's group wanted a photo beside the school garden. She was happy to help hold the sign, but she did not want to be in the picture. When the camera came out, she said, "No thanks. I'll take the photo."

Two classmates laughed and said, "Come on, don't be weird." Priya repeated, "I said no," and stepped out of the frame. The teacher heard her and reminded the group that a refusal did not need a long explanation. The students took the photo without Priya. They did not crop in her shoulder later or add a mean caption. Respecting no meant changing the plan.

A little later, Dev asked if he could borrow Priya's markers for the poster. Priya said yes, but added, "Only during class, and please keep the purple one with the set." Dev agreed. He used the markers carefully and returned every one before the bell. Her yes had a limit, and the limit mattered.

During health class, students practised short refusal lines: "No thanks." "Stop." "I don't want to." They also practised respectful replies: "Okay." "Thanks for telling me." "I'll change the plan." Mr. Alvarez explained that asking, waiting, and respecting the answer were skills for ordinary situations, such as photos, borrowing belongings, games, and personal space.

Priya noticed that consent was not a trick to get the answer someone wanted. It was permission that a person could give, limit, change, or refuse. A yes to one thing was not a yes to everything. A no did not make someone rude.

Before dismissal, the classmates who had teased her apologized. Priya accepted the apology after they showed what they had learned: they asked before taking another photo and waited for the answer. Respecting people's choices helped everyone feel safer, included, and in control of their own boundaries.`,activity:`Sort scenes into ask first / it’s a yes / it’s a no / tell an adult. Write one refusal line and one respect line.`,worksheetTitle:`Yes, no, ask, tell`,fields:[{kind:`table`,id:`scenes`,columns:[`Scene`,`Ask first / Yes / No / Tell an adult`,`Why`],rowLabels:[`Someone wants to post a photo of you`,`A friend asks to borrow your hoodie for recess and you say yes`,`Someone keeps tickling after you said stop`,`A classmate wants a hug and you do not`,`An adult asks you to keep a special secret about touching`]},{kind:`text`,id:`refuse`,label:`One short refusal line I can use`},{kind:`text`,id:`respect`,label:`One line that shows I heard no`},{kind:`exit`,id:`exit`,prompt:`Why is silence not the same as yes?`}],sample:{scenes:`Ask first — their image, their permission. | Yes — clear agreement with a limit (recess). | No, then tell an adult if it continues — stop means stop. | No — you do not have to hug. | Tell an adult the same day — unsafe secret.`,refuse:`No thanks. Stop. I don’t want to.`,respect:`Okay. Thanks for telling me.`,exit:`Silence, freeze, or “I guess” under pressure is not a clear yes. Responsibility is to wait for a real yes or to accept a no.`},checks:[`Stop after no is named as the required move?`,`Unsafe secret sorted to tell an adult?`,`Stayed on Grade 5 contexts (space, photos, games, borrowing, digital)?`],tip:`If jokes start, reset once and continue. Consent lessons stay school-appropriate. Sexual activity is not this unit.`,preview:`Weeks 19–24: Healthy Eating — Food Guide plate, body systems, FNMI food knowledge.`}],r=[{week:19,unitId:`nutrition`,title:`Canada’s Food Guide Plate (2019)`,focus:`Half / quarter / quarter`,outcomeFocus:`Use the 2019 plate, not the old rainbow. AHS Grade 5 Finding Nutritional Balance is a valid resource.`,materials:[`Week 19 worksheet`,`Optional Food Guide plate on projector`],knowledge:[`Canada’s Food Guide 2019 recommends a plate: ½ vegetables and fruits, ¼ whole grain foods, ¼ protein foods; water as the drink of choice.`,`How we eat matters: meals together when we can, cook more often. One meal is not a panic.`],understanding:[`A plate of proportions is easier to picture at one meal than a rainbow of servings.`],skills:[`Evaluate meals using Canada’s Food Guide plate.`],warmUp:`Draw a circle. Cross out the old rainbow kindly. We use 2019 proportions at a meal.`,teachTitle:`Read the plate`,teachSteps:[`Half: vegetables and fruits (fresh, frozen, or canned).`,`One quarter: whole grain foods (oats, brown/wild rice, whole grain pasta or bread).`,`One quarter: protein foods (beans, lentils, nuts, eggs, fish, lean meat, lower-fat dairy, fortified soy). Plant proteins more often.`,`Water is the drink of choice.`,`How we eat matters: meals together when we can, cook more often, no panic about one meal.`,`Do not comment on anyone’s lunch. Access is not equal.`],story:`Leila was helping plan a class picnic when she remembered the food poster in the hallway. It showed a rainbow of food groups, but she was not sure how to use it for one actual meal. Her teacher invited everyone to sketch a plate instead. The simple picture had three parts: half vegetables and fruits, one quarter whole grain foods, and one quarter protein foods. Water sat beside the plate as the drink of choice.

Leila tested the idea with a lunch she might really eat. She drew whole-grain toast in the grain section, a scrambled egg in the protein section, and apple slices in the vegetables-and-fruits section. Then she paused. Her plate had fruit, but no vegetable. That was not a failure; it was just useful information. She added a small container of leftover carrots and frozen peas from home. Frozen and canned vegetables and fruits can count too, and foods do not have to look fancy to nourish someone.

At the picnic, Leila noticed that classmates had different foods, budgets, cultures, allergies, and amounts of time for packing lunch. No one inspected anyone else's container. The plate was a way to picture balance, not a rule for judging people. As she filled her water bottle, Leila realized the model gave her a practical question to ask: What is one food I could add or switch today? A helpful plan can be flexible, realistic, and kind.`,activity:`Plate A = a meal they actually eat. Plate B = one realistic change.`,worksheetTitle:`What’s on the plate?`,fields:[{kind:`intro`,text:`2019 plate: ½ vegetables and fruits · ¼ whole grains · ¼ protein. Water to drink.`},{kind:`table`,id:`plateA`,columns:[`Food`,`Veg/fruit, whole grain, protein, water, or other`],rows:4},{kind:`choice`,id:`close`,label:`How close to half / quarter / quarter?`,options:[`Close`,`Partly`,`Not yet`]},{kind:`text`,id:`plateB`,label:`Plate B — one realistic change (not a fantasy chef meal)`,rows:2},{kind:`exit`,id:`exit`,prompt:`Why did Canada switch from a rainbow of servings to a plate of proportions?`}],sample:{plateA:`Whole-grain toast — whole grain. Scrambled egg — protein. Apple slices — veg/fruit. Water — water.`,close:`Partly`,plateB:`Add leftover carrots or frozen mixed vegetables on the side. Frozen and canned count.`,exit:`It is easier to picture at one meal, and it puts vegetables and fruits at half without counting cups in the cafeteria.`},checks:[`Can they point to half / quarter / quarter?`,`Frozen/canned treated as valid?`],tip:`Never grade a lunch kit. Praise one small swap they control.`,preview:`Next week: food and water meeting body systems.`},{week:20,unitId:`nutrition`,title:`Nutrition, Hydration, and How the Body Uses Food`,focus:`Energy and nourishment`,outcomeFocus:`Food is fuel, building, and repair. Hydration supports every system. Personal factors change needs.`,materials:[`Week 20 worksheet`],knowledge:[`Nutrition provides energy and nourishment to the body.`,`Carbohydrates fuel. Protein builds and repairs. Fats help absorb some vitamins. Fibre helps digestion. Water carries nutrients and cools you.`,`Personal factors influence nutrition needs (activity, growth, allergies, culture).`],understanding:[`Nutrition contributes to well-being — and it is not the only factor. Food is not a moral test.`],skills:[`Examine how nutrition and hydration support the body.`],warmUp:`What does your body do with lunch besides “not be hungry”? Collect: energy, growth, repair, thinking.`,teachTitle:`Food is more than calories`,teachSteps:[`Carbohydrates fuel. Protein builds and repairs. Fats help absorb some vitamins. Fibre helps digestion. Vitamins/minerals are small but essential.`,`Water carries nutrients, cools you, helps blood move. Thirst is a late signal.`,`Needs differ: soccer night vs reading night; growth spurts; allergies; cultural fasting. Those are factors, not “picky.”`,`Food is not a moral test (good kid / bad kid). No calorie counting in Grade 5.`],story:`Marcus usually thought of lunch as the meal that stopped his stomach from rumbling. During Health class, he tried a different question: What does his body do with lunch after he eats it? That afternoon he had soccer practice, followed by a math quiz, so the answer felt important. Food could provide energy for moving, materials for growth and repair, and nutrients that helped his brain and body work. It was more than a number called calories.

He made a pretend map from his lunch to different jobs. Vegetables and fruits could provide vitamins, minerals, and fibre. Whole grain foods could provide energy that lasted through a busy afternoon. Protein foods, such as beans, yogurt, eggs, fish, tofu, or chicken, could help build and repair body tissues. His water bottle helped replace fluid his body used while breathing, sweating, and carrying out everyday jobs. Water also helped him feel ready for class and practice.

Marcus noticed that needs and choices are shaped by real life. On a soccer night, he might need a more filling snack than on a quiet reading afternoon. A classmate with an allergy needed different ingredients, so sharing food without checking was not safe. That was not being picky; it was caring for health. Marcus wrote one final note: food can support energy and mood, but nutrition is not the only part of well-being. Sleep, movement, safety, friendships, and feelings matter too. He left for practice thinking about nourishment, not judgment.`,activity:`Match plate sections to jobs; note one personal factor.`,worksheetTitle:`Fuel, fluids, and factors`,fields:[{kind:`table`,id:`jobs`,columns:[`This…`,`Main job in the body (your words)`],rowLabels:[`Vegetables and fruits`,`Whole grain foods`,`Protein foods`,`Water`]},{kind:`text`,id:`factor`,label:`One personal factor that changes needs`},{kind:`text`,id:`activity`,label:`High-activity day vs low-activity day — food or water`},{kind:`exit`,id:`exit`,prompt:`To what extent does nutrition contribute to well-being? Two sentences, one example.`}],sample:{jobs:`Veg/fruit: vitamins, fibre, digestion. Grains: longer-lasting energy. Protein: building and repair. Water: carries nutrients, cools you. Thirst is a late signal.`,factor:`Soccer night versus a reading night, or an allergy that means we do not share.`,activity:`High-activity: extra water bottle and a protein-plus-fruit snack. Low-activity: still eat a regular meal; I do not skip just because I sat more.`,exit:`A lot — energy and mood for afternoon math. Example: water plus a grain-and-protein lunch keeps me from fading. Sleep and safety still matter too.`},checks:[`More than “food gives energy”?`,`Water treated as essential?`],tip:`If weight-loss talk starts: redirect to nourishment and patterns, not shrinking bodies.`,preview:`Next week: supporting the heart and digestion.`},{week:21,unitId:`nutrition`,title:`Supporting the Heart and Digestion`,focus:`Cardiovascular and digestive systems`,outcomeFocus:`Nutrition and hydration support the cardiovascular and digestive systems. Patterns matter more than one meal.`,materials:[`Week 21 worksheet`],knowledge:[`The digestive system breaks food down. Fibre and water help waste move.`,`The cardiovascular system (heart and vessels) moves oxygen and nutrients.`],understanding:[`Everyday patterns of vegetables, fruit, whole grains, protein, and water support these systems. One birthday cake does not ruin a heart.`],skills:[`Examine how nutrition and hydration support body systems.`],warmUp:`Fist on chest (heart is a muscle). Trace mouth → stomach → intestines.`,teachTitle:`Two systems, one lunch`,teachSteps:[`Digestive: breaks food down. Fibre + water help waste move. Eating slowly helps.`,`Cardiovascular: heart and vessels move oxygen and nutrients. Everyday pattern of veg/fruit, whole grains, protein, water supports it. Sugary drinks and very salty snacks as the usual pattern make the system work harder.`,`One birthday cake does not ruin a heart. Patterns matter.`,`We do not diagnose from a Health lesson. Talk in often versus sometimes.`],story:`Anika placed one hand over her chest and listened quietly for her heartbeat. Her heart was a muscle, working day and night to pump blood around her body. Then she traced an imaginary path from her mouth to her stomach and intestines. The digestive system broke food into smaller parts, absorbed useful nutrients, and moved what the body did not need. Her teacher smiled at the route. One lunch could involve more than one body system at once.

At recess, Anika and her partner compared two made-up snack patterns. The first was an apple with a handful of nuts or seeds, if a person could safely eat them. It offered fibre and protein and could fit into an overall pattern of nourishing foods. The second was a large sugary drink and chips every day. Those foods could be enjoyed sometimes, but using that combination as the usual snack would leave less room for foods that supplied other nutrients and fibre. The point was not to label a person or a food as good or bad.

Why did water appear in their notes about both systems? Fluids help food move through digestion, and blood carries oxygen and nutrients as the heart pumps. Anika also remembered that people have different needs, allergies, routines, and access to food. A Health lesson could explain how systems work, but it could not diagnose anyone from a snack. She underlined the words often and sometimes. Looking at patterns over time, with kindness and curiosity, was more useful than judging one lunch.`,activity:`Systems table + two-snack compare.`,worksheetTitle:`Heart, gut, and a snack`,fields:[{kind:`table`,id:`sys`,columns:[`System`,`What it does`,`A habit that supports it`],rowLabels:[`Digestive`,`Cardiovascular (heart and blood)`]},{kind:`table`,id:`snack`,columns:[`Snack`,`Helps digestion?`,`Helps the heart over time?`],rowLabels:[`Apple + handful of nuts`,`Large sugary drink + chips most days after school`]},{kind:`exit`,id:`exit`,prompt:`Why does water show up on both lists?`}],sample:{sys:`Digestive: breaks food down; fibre plus water; eat slowly, include fruit or veg. Cardiovascular: heart and vessels move oxygen and nutrients; everyday plate pattern.`,snack:`Apple + nuts: yes and yes as an often snack. Sugary drink + chips most days: not a helpful usual pattern.`,exit:`Water helps digestion move, and it helps blood carry what the heart is pumping.`},checks:[`Patterns vs one meal named?`,`No diagnosing classmates?`],tip:`Keep language in often / sometimes. Never label a student’s home food as bad.`,preview:`Next week: muscles, bones, and reproductive health — still about nourishment.`},{week:22,unitId:`nutrition`,title:`Supporting Muscles, Bones, and Reproductive Health`,focus:`Musculoskeletal and reproductive systems`,outcomeFocus:`Nutrition and hydration also support the musculoskeletal system and a healthy reproductive system as the body matures. Stay on nourishment, not shrinking bodies.`,materials:[`Week 22 worksheet`],knowledge:[`The musculoskeletal system (muscles and bones) needs protein, calcium-rich foods, vitamin D (food + safe daylight), water, and activity.`,`Nutrition supports the reproductive system as it matures during puberty (sleep, food, activity — not special “puberty diets”).`],understanding:[`The same Food Guide pattern supports more than one system.`],skills:[`Examine how nutrition and hydration support body systems.`],warmUp:`Flex a forearm. Tap a shin. Those are muscle and bone. Ask: what did breakfast have to do with gym class?`,teachTitle:`Nourish the frame — and the systems that mature`,teachSteps:[`Muscles use protein for repair after activity. Bones use calcium-rich foods (dairy, fortified plant drinks, canned fish with bones, some greens) plus activity and vitamin D.`,`Water helps muscles work and recover. “I forgot my bottle” is a well-being issue on a soccer day.`,`The reproductive system is also a body system. As it matures in puberty, the same pattern helps: regular meals, iron-rich foods, water, rest. We do not teach special shrinking diets.`,`Iron-rich foods (meat, beans, lentils, fortified cereal, greens) support blood. This matters for growing bodies, including people who menstruate — name it calmly, no scare stories. Details of menstruation return in Week 28.`],story:`After intramurals, Noah sat on the gym bench while his calves trembled. He had sprinted hard, and his muscles were tired. At lunch he chose chili with beans, a whole grain bun, and water. His teacher explained that protein foods such as beans, eggs, yogurt, tofu, fish, or meat provide materials the body can use to build and repair tissues. Protein was not a prize for athletes or a luxury food. It was one part of ordinary nourishment.

The class talked about bones next. Bones gave the body support and protected important organs. Calcium-rich foods, such as milk, fortified soy beverage, yogurt, cheese, tofu made with calcium, and some canned fish, could help support bones as they grew. Weight-bearing movement, like walking, running, dancing, and playing games, also challenged bones and muscles in healthy ways. Rest mattered too. Sleep gave the body time to recover, learn, and manage the many changes of growing.

Then the teacher added the reproductive system to the class chart. A few students giggled, so Noah looked down at his notebook. The teacher calmly reminded everyone that the reproductive system was one of the body systems that could mature during adolescence. It deserved the same respectful science language as the heart or stomach. Food, water, movement, sleep, and support help the whole body, including systems that are changing. None of those choices makes a person look older, and no one's body is a classroom comment. Noah closed his notebook with a clear idea: caring for health is about supporting the body, not ranking it.`,activity:`Match systems to foods and habits. One respectful sentence about why we do not diet-talk in Grade 5 Health.`,worksheetTitle:`Frame and systems`,fields:[{kind:`table`,id:`sys`,columns:[`System`,`What it needs (food / water / habit)`,`One example I could actually eat or do`],rowLabels:[`Muscles`,`Bones`,`Reproductive system (maturing)`]},{kind:`text`,id:`iron`,label:`One iron-rich food (meat, beans, lentils, fortified cereal, greens) I know`},{kind:`exit`,id:`exit`,prompt:`Why do we talk about nourishing a maturing body, not about shrinking a body, in Grade 5?`}],sample:{sys:`Muscles: protein + water after activity — chili beans. Bones: calcium-rich food + gym — fortified soy drink or milk, and actually moving. Reproductive: regular meals, iron-rich foods, sleep, water — the same plate, not a special diet.`,iron:`Lentils in soup, or fortified cereal, or beef in a taco.`,exit:`The outcome is well-being and body systems. Shrinking talk is not Grade 5 PEW and it harms people whose bodies are changing at different speeds.`},checks:[`Muscles and bones connected to food AND activity?`,`Reproductive system named as a system, without pregnancy/birth content?`],tip:`If body-size comments start, shut them down once and return to systems. Growth unit will handle puberty with more care in Weeks 25–30.`,preview:`Next week: cultural, traditional, and medicinal food practices, including First Nations, Métis, and Inuit.`},{week:23,unitId:`nutrition`,title:`Cultural, Traditional, and Medicinal Food Practices`,focus:`Including FNMI food knowledge`,outcomeFocus:`Cultural, traditional, and medicinal food practices, including First Nations, Métis, and Inuit, contribute to well-being. Respect, not a recipe test.`,materials:[`Week 23 worksheet`,`Optional: a locally appropriate, school-approved FNMI resource (not a random internet list)`],knowledge:[`Food practices can be cultural, traditional, and medicinal.`,`First Nations, Métis, and Inuit food knowledge includes relationships with land, water, animals, and plants — not only a grocery list.`,`Valid learning comes from respectful sources: Knowledge Keepers, Elders, school-approved resources — not stereotypes.`],understanding:[`Respect for diverse food practices supports belonging and well-being.`,`Canada’s Food Guide includes First Nations, Inuit, and Métis resources that sit beside the plate, not under it.`],skills:[`Investigate cultural, traditional, and medicinal food practices, including First Nations, Métis, and Inuit.`],warmUp:`Name one food that means something in your family (celebration, comfort, everyday). No ranking. No “weird food” talk.`,teachTitle:`Food is knowledge, not a costume`,teachSteps:[`Many families have cultural food practices: what is eaten, when, with whom, and how it is prepared. These can support belonging and well-being.`,`Traditional practices are passed down. Medicinal practices use foods or plants as part of staying well — this is knowledge, not a Grade 5 diagnosis kit.`,`First Nations, Métis, and Inuit food knowledge in Alberta may include (examples, not a complete list): berries, fish, moose or other hunted foods, bannock, tea, gardens, and teachings about taking only what is needed. Specific nations (Cree, Dene, Blackfoot, Métis, and others) have their own knowledge. Do not flatten them into one “Native food.”`,`Canada’s Food Guide has complementary First Nations, Inuit, and Métis healthy eating resources. The 2019 plate is one tool. It is not the only valid way to eat.`,`We listen with respect. We do not treat sacred or medicinal knowledge as a craft activity. We do not require students to share family food if they do not want to.`],story:`On Friday evenings, Leila's family often shared rice at the table. Sometimes it was served with vegetables, sometimes with a stew, and sometimes with foods connected to a celebration. The meal carried memories of relatives, conversations, and a weekly rhythm. Leila did not need to announce that her family's rice was a cultural practice. She knew it from living it.

In class, students made two columns in their notebooks. One was called "What I know from my family," and the other was "What I am learning with respect." A school-approved resource from a guest Knowledge Keeper described how some Métis families have smoked fish and how some Cree teachings connect berries with seasons, land, and relationships. The words some and respect mattered. No single example could stand for every family or Nation, and the class was not being invited to copy a tradition for a costume or a theme day.

Leila wrote that her family's Friday rice belonged in the first column. Bannock in a textbook was not hers to claim as her own tradition, but she could learn about its history and listen carefully when people shared their knowledge. She also learned that medicinal food and plant knowledge require responsibility. A plant on a field trip was not automatically safe to touch, eat, or use. Traditional knowledge is held and shared by people with the right relationship, experience, and permission.

When the class packed up, Leila understood that food can be nourishment, history, identity, and connection. Respect meant asking, listening, and avoiding claims that were not hers to make.`,activity:`Respect notes: one family practice (optional), one FNMI idea learned with respect, one “I will not” (stereotype or taking knowledge).`,worksheetTitle:`Food, culture, and respect`,fields:[{kind:`text`,id:`family`,label:`Optional: one food practice from my family or community (you may write “private”)`,rows:2},{kind:`text`,id:`learn`,label:`One First Nations, Métis, or Inuit food teaching I can state respectfully (from class, not from a joke or a movie)`,rows:2},{kind:`text`,id:`plate`,label:`How can traditional foods sit beside the Food Guide plate instead of being ranked as “less healthy”?`,rows:2},{kind:`exit`,id:`exit`,prompt:`What is the difference between respecting a food practice and copying it as a costume?`}],sample:{family:`Friday rice with the people I live with. Celebration food, not a contest.`,learn:`Some Métis and First Nations families have teachings about fish, berries, and taking only what is needed. Nations have their own knowledge — not one “Native food.”`,plate:`Traditional foods can be vegetables, grains, protein, and water too. The plate is a tool. It does not rank someone’s culture.`,exit:`Respect is listening and not ranking. Copying is taking something that is not mine and using it as a costume or a craft without context or permission.`},checks:[`No stereotype language?`,`FNMI knowledge treated as knowledge, not a recipe test?`],tip:`If you do not have a local Knowledge Keeper, use school-approved CFG / FNMI resources. Do not improvise sacred content. Invite, do not extract.`,preview:`Next week: finding nutritional balance — assessing meals with a valid resource.`},{week:24,unitId:`nutrition`,title:`Finding Nutritional Balance — Assessing Meals`,focus:`Unit wrap · AHS resource`,outcomeFocus:`Assess nutritional balance of meals using valid resources (CFG 2019, AHS Grade 5 Finding Nutritional Balance). Answer the guiding question.`,materials:[`Week 24 worksheet`,`Optional: AHS Grade 5 Finding Nutritional Balance (valid support)`,`Unit rubric`],knowledge:[`Valid resources help us assess meals: Canada’s Food Guide 2019, Alberta Health Services Grade 5 Finding Nutritional Balance.`,`Balance is a pattern over time, not a perfect plate at every sitting.`],understanding:[`Nutrition contributes to health and well-being of body systems — to an important extent — alongside sleep, safety, belonging, and activity.`],skills:[`Assess nutritional balance of meals using valid resources.`],warmUp:`Post the guiding question: To what extent does nutrition contribute to health and well-being? Two silent sentences, then two shares.`,teachTitle:`Assess, don’t shame`,teachSteps:[`Use the plate: half / quarter / quarter + water. Frozen and canned count. Cultural foods count.`,`Ask: often or sometimes? One meal is data, not a verdict on a family.`,`Name which systems a balanced pattern supports: digestive, cardiovascular, musculoskeletal, reproductive (maturing).`,`Answer the guiding question with a claim + example + a limit (nutrition is not the only factor).`],story:`Marcus's teacher placed two sample meal cards on the table. She reminded the class that the cards were not anyone's lunch kit. They were simply clues for practising how to assess a meal without shaming a person. Marcus picked up the first card. It showed leftover stew with carrots, barley, and beans, plus a glass of water. He could see vegetables, a whole grain, and a protein food in one warm bowl. It was close to the plate model, even though it did not look like a picture from a magazine.

The second card showed a granola bar and a sugary drink on a rushed morning. Marcus knew that mornings could go sideways. Someone might miss the bus, feel unwell, have limited food at home, or simply be in a hurry. The class did not call the meal bad. Instead, they considered a next step that might be possible: add a piece of fruit and water tomorrow, or include a more filling food when there was time. A single meal did not tell the story of a person's health.

The teacher asked, "To what extent can food support well-being?" Marcus answered that it could support energy, concentration, mood, growth, and the work of body systems to a significant extent. Then he added a limit. Sleep, physical activity, safety, stress, belonging, culture, access, and caring relationships also affect well-being. Nutrition mattered, but it was not a moral test or the only answer. Marcus turned over the cards and wrote, "Assess the pattern; support the person." That sounded more helpful than a shame speech.`,activity:`Assess two sample meals (provided). Then one meal they eat. Then the guiding-question paragraph. Score the unit rubric once.`,worksheetTitle:`Balance check`,fields:[{kind:`table`,id:`meals`,columns:[`Meal`,`Close / partly / not yet`,`One realistic change`],rowLabels:[`Stew with carrots, barley, beans + water`,`Granola bar + sugary drink on a rushed morning`]},{kind:`text`,id:`mine`,label:`A meal I actually eat — how close, and one change I control`,rows:2},{kind:`text`,id:`systems`,label:`Name two body systems a balanced pattern supports, in your words`},{kind:`exit`,id:`exit`,prompt:`To what extent does nutrition contribute to health and well-being? Claim + example + one limit.`}],sample:{meals:`Stew: close — veg, grain, protein, water. | Granola bar + sugary drink: not yet as a usual breakfast; add fruit and water next time, no shame.`,mine:`Toast, egg, apple — partly; add leftover carrots. Frozen counts.`,systems:`Digestive (fibre and water) and cardiovascular (everyday plate pattern, water).`,exit:`A lot — energy and mood for afternoon math, and support for heart, gut, muscles, and a maturing body. Limit: sleep and safety still matter, and one rushed morning is not a verdict.`},checks:[`Guiding question answered with a limit?`,`No lunch-kit shaming?`],tip:`AHS Finding Nutritional Balance is a valid support if your school has it. CFG 2019 plate is enough if not.`,preview:`Weeks 25–30: Growth & Development. Send the family letter at least one month before Week 25.`}],i=[{week:25,unitId:`growth`,title:`Maturation and Changes in Adolescence`,focus:`Physical, cognitive, behavioural`,outcomeFocus:`Adolescence is the stage between childhood and adulthood. Maturation is the process of growing and developing. Changes are physical, cognitive (thinking), and behavioural. Timing varies. No one is “late” as a joke.`,materials:[`Week 25 worksheet`,`Question box`],knowledge:[`Adolescence is the life stage between childhood and adulthood.`,`Maturation is the process of growing and developing.`,`Changes during adolescence are physical, cognitive, and behavioural.`],understanding:[`Everyone’s timeline is different. Comparing bodies in class is not allowed.`],skills:[`Investigate maturation and identify changes during adolescence.`],warmUp:`Three columns: Body / Thinking / Behaviour. Students add one change they have noticed in general (not pointing at a classmate).`,teachTitle:`Adolescence is a range, not a race`,teachSteps:[`Physical: growth spurts, sleep need, appetite, sweating, skin, body shape, hair in new places. These connect to hormones — chemical messengers.`,`Cognitive: longer-term thinking starts to grow; you can still feel very in-the-moment.`,`Behavioural: want more independence, care more what peers think, mood can swing. That is common, not a character flaw.`,`Everyone’s timeline is different. Comparing bodies in class is not allowed.`,`Questions go in the box. We answer facts next class. We do not answer personal questions about anyone in the room.`],story:`Ibrahim was adding ideas to the board when the class began a lesson about adolescence. Under Body, he wrote that many young people need more sleep and may notice changes in height, shape, skin, or strength. Under Thinking, he wrote that a group project could suddenly feel much more important because friendships and belonging mattered deeply. Under Behaviour, he wrote that someone might want a closed door while doing homework or might begin making more decisions independently. These were examples, not a checklist that every person had to follow.

The teacher explained that adolescence is the stage between childhood and adulthood, while maturation is the process of growing and developing. The timing and order of changes vary widely. Some people begin earlier, some later, and some changes happen gradually. No one is late, strange, or behind because their body follows its own schedule.

During partner work, Ibrahim heard a whisper from the back table. A student had started ranking who had already had a growth spurt. Ibrahim looked uncomfortable and asked, "How would it feel to have people keeping score about your body?" The room went quiet. The teacher thanked him and reset the conversation in a calm science voice. It was fine to be curious about development; it was not fine to turn classmates into examples or compare their bodies.

A few students still giggled, and the teacher did not make a spectacle of them. She simply returned to the facts and reminded everyone that questions could be private. Ibrahim added one sentence to the board: "Adolescence is a range, not a race." The class left with room for every timeline.`,activity:`Sort changes and write one respectful sentence about different timelines.`,worksheetTitle:`Changes during adolescence`,fields:[{kind:`table`,id:`changes`,columns:[`Change`,`Physical, thinking, or behaviour?`,`A supportive response`],rowLabels:[`Needing more sleep`,`Caring more what friends think`,`Growth spurt / clothes don't fit`,`Wanting a closed bedroom door more often`]},{kind:`text`,id:`rank`,label:`Why is it unkind to rank who “hit puberty” first?`},{kind:`exit`,id:`exit`,prompt:`Name one physical, one thinking, and one behaviour change that can happen in adolescence.`}],sample:{changes:`Sleep — physical — do not tease; growing bodies need rest. | Friends — thinking/behaviour — normal; still use your values. | Clothes — physical — different timelines, no ranking. | Door — behaviour — privacy can be independence; families can still check in.`,rank:`Bodies are not a race. Ranking makes people feel watched. Timing varies. Comparing bodies in class is not allowed.`,exit:`Physical: needing more sleep. Thinking: caring more about a group project. Behaviour: wanting a closed door for homework.`},checks:[`Can they use the three categories?`,`No pointing at classmates?`],tip:`Giggles are normal. Reset once (“we use calm science voices”) and continue. Do not shame.`,preview:`Next week: physical changes of puberty with correct words, calmly.`},{week:26,unitId:`growth`,title:`Physical Changes During Puberty`,focus:`Hormones and secondary characteristics`,outcomeFocus:`Puberty is the set of physical changes that lead toward adult reproductive capacity. Hormones increase. Secondary sex characteristics appear. Timing varies.`,materials:[`Week 26 worksheet`,`Question box`],knowledge:[`Hormones are chemical messengers. The pituitary gland signals ovaries or testicles to make more hormones (estrogen, progesterone, testosterone).`,`Secondary sex characteristics include breast development, voice deepening, hair in new places, wider hips or shoulders, more oil and sweat, growth spurt.`],understanding:[`People notice different mixes and timings. Growth speed does not tell you character or worth.`],skills:[`Identify changes during adolescence connected to puberty and hormones.`],warmUp:`Define hormone in one kid sentence: a chemical message that tells the body to change or do a job.`,teachTitle:`What puberty does`,teachSteps:[`Pituitary gland in the brain signals ovaries or testicles to make more hormones (estrogen, progesterone, testosterone — names on the board, not a memorization test).`,`Primary changes: reproductive organs mature (next two weeks).`,`Secondary characteristics: breast development; voice deepening; hair under arms, on legs, on the face, and in the pubic area; wider hips or wider shoulders; more oil on skin; more sweat; growth spurt.`,`People may notice different mixes and timings. Stick to facts. No commenting on a classmate’s body.`,`Voice cracks, extra laundry, and needing deodorant are normal. Hygiene is respect for yourself and others.`],story:`Sophie was practising how to explain the word hormone without making it sound mysterious. She wrote, "A hormone is a chemical messenger that tells cells or organs to change or do a job." The class learned the names estrogen, progesterone, and testosterone. The teacher said the names were useful science vocabulary, not a memorization contest and not labels for deciding what someone was supposed to be like.

Next came a fact-and-myth activity. Sophie read the first card: "Everyone starts puberty at the same age." The class marked it as a myth. Puberty begins and progresses on different timelines. The next card said, "Hormones are chemical messengers." Fact. "Sweat and skin oil often increase during puberty." Fact. "You can tell everything about a person from how quickly they grow." Myth. Development has many parts, and an outside observer cannot know someone's whole story by looking at them.

The teacher described some secondary characteristics in neutral, respectful language. A person's voice may change, body hair may increase, skin may become oilier, and body shape may change. People may need deodorant, more frequent washing, or extra laundry. These changes are ordinary body processes, not reasons to tease someone. Hygiene can help a person feel comfortable and show consideration for shared spaces, but it is not a measure of worth.

When a student asked whether everyone would have exactly the same changes, the teacher answered no. Families, cultures, health, genetics, and timing all make development individual. Sophie closed her notebook. The lesson had not made bodies into a checklist. It had given the class accurate words, privacy, and permission to grow at its own pace.`,activity:`Fact / myth table plus two general physical changes (not naming a classmate).`,worksheetTitle:`Puberty facts`,fields:[{kind:`table`,id:`fm`,columns:[`Statement`,`Fact or myth?`,`Why`],rowLabels:[`Everyone starts puberty at the same age.`,`Hormones are chemical messengers.`,`Sweat and skin oil often increase.`,`You can tell everything about a person from how fast they grow.`]},{kind:`text`,id:`two`,label:`Two physical changes I might notice or that a friend might notice (general, not naming a classmate)`,rows:2},{kind:`exit`,id:`exit`,prompt:`Who is a trusted adult I could ask a puberty question?`}],sample:{fm:`Myth — timing varies. | Fact — they tell the body to change or do a job. | Fact — secondary characteristics can include more oil and sweat. | Myth — growth speed is not character, smarts, or worth.`,two:`A growth spurt that makes clothes short, and needing deodorant because sweat increases. General facts, not a person in this room.`,exit:`A parent or guardian, or my teacher. Questions can also go in the class question box.`},checks:[`Hormones defined simply?`,`Myths about same-age start and judging bodies busted?`],tip:`If a student is visibly anxious, offer to complete the sheet in a quieter space. Same content.`,preview:`Next: organs and what they do — still no pregnancy/birth sequence.`},{week:27,unitId:`growth`,title:`Puberty and Reproductive Capacity`,focus:`Organs and functions`,outcomeFocus:`Each part of the human reproductive system has a job. Puberty matures those parts so the body becomes capable of reproduction in the future. We name parts and functions. The fertilization-through-pregnancy-and-birth sequence is Grade 6.`,materials:[`Week 27 worksheet`,`Simple unlabeled diagrams if your school provides them — or word lists only`],knowledge:[`Each part of the human reproductive system serves a specific function.`,`Typical female system: ovaries store egg cells; uterus is a muscular organ with a lining; vagina is a canal; vulva is the outside parts; fallopian tubes carry an egg toward the uterus.`,`Typical male system: testicles produce sperm cells and testosterone; scrotum holds the testicles; penis delivers urine and, later, semen.`,`The human reproductive system and other body systems are interconnected.`],understanding:[`Having these organs mature means the body is gaining the capacity for human reproduction. Capacity is not the same as being ready to parent, and it is not a request to discuss sexual activity.`],skills:[`Identify the components of the human reproductive system.`,`Describe the functions of the components of the human reproductive system.`],warmUp:`Rules: correct words, calm voice, no jokes about anyone in the room. Pass is allowed.`,teachTitle:`Parts have jobs`,teachSteps:[`Female system (typical): ovaries store egg cells; uterus is a muscular organ with a lining; vagina is a canal from the uterus to the outside; vulva is the outside parts. Fallopian tubes carry an egg toward the uterus — name them. Do not teach the fertilization-to-birth storyline; that sequence is Grade 6.`,`Male system (typical): testicles produce sperm cells and testosterone; scrotum holds the testicles; penis delivers urine and, later, semen.`,`These systems connect to other systems (hormones, blood). Growing them is part of overall maturation.`,`Capacity means the body is becoming able, in the future, to produce egg or sperm cells. It is not the same as being ready to parent.`,`Drawings stay scientific. No phones out. Use correct words: penis, testicles, vulva, vagina, uterus, ovaries.`],story:`Before the lesson began, Ravi noticed three reminders on the board: use correct words, speak calmly, and do not make jokes about anyone in the room. The teacher also said that students could pass on sharing a personal question. The class was learning anatomy, not discussing anyone's body or private life.

Ravi worked with a diagram and matched each part with its basic job. Ovaries store egg cells. The uterus is a muscular organ with a lining. The vagina is a canal from the uterus to the outside of the body, and the vulva is the name for the outside parts. Testicles produce sperm cells and testosterone. The scrotum holds the testicles. The penis is an organ that carries urine out of the body and, after puberty, can carry semen out as well. These were body parts and functions, stated in the same straightforward way the class had used for the heart and lungs.

A student asked why the class needed exact anatomical words instead of slang. The teacher explained that slang can turn a science lesson into a joke contest, and it can embarrass or exclude people. Correct words make questions clearer and help people communicate with trusted adults or health professionals when they need information.

The teacher then explained that as reproductive organs mature, a body may gain the biological capacity for human reproduction. Capacity is not the same as emotional readiness, social readiness, or being ready to parent. It is also not an invitation to discuss sexual activity. The class stayed with organs and functions; later lessons would cover other topics at the appropriate time.

Ravi left with a simple conclusion: accurate language keeps a sensitive lesson respectful. Everyone's development has its own timeline, and no one in the room was a science exhibit.`,activity:`Match organ → job. Short “capacity vs ready” sentence.`,worksheetTitle:`Organs and jobs`,fields:[{kind:`table`,id:`organs`,columns:[`Part`,`Job in your own words`],rowLabels:[`Ovaries`,`Uterus`,`Vagina / vulva`,`Testicles`,`Penis`,`Scrotum`]},{kind:`text`,id:`capacity`,label:`What does “capacity for human reproduction” mean in Grade 5 language?`,rows:2},{kind:`text`,id:`not`,label:`What are we not learning this year (Grade 6 content)?`},{kind:`exit`,id:`exit`,prompt:`Why do we use correct anatomical words instead of slang?`}],sample:{organs:`Ovaries: store egg cells. Uterus: muscular organ with a lining. Vagina: canal; vulva: outside parts. Testicles: sperm and testosterone. Penis: urine and, later, semen. Scrotum: holds the testicles.`,capacity:`The body is becoming able, in the future, to produce egg or sperm cells. That is not the same as being ready to parent, and we are not studying sexual activity.`,not:`The fertilization-through-pregnancy-and-birth sequence, contraception, and dating advice. That is Grade 6 and later.`,exit:`Slang turns the lesson into a joke contest and can humiliate people. Correct words keep it science.`},checks:[`Organs matched to jobs with correct words?`,`Capacity distinguished from “ready to parent”?`,`No Grade 6 pregnancy/birth sequence?`],tip:`If your school provides diagrams, keep them scientific and unlabeled until students fill jobs. No phones. Pass is allowed.`,preview:`Next week: menstruation and sperm production as signs of reproductive capacity.`},{week:28,unitId:`growth`,title:`Menstruation and Sperm Production`,focus:`Signs of reproductive capacity`,outcomeFocus:`Menstruation and sperm production are signs that the reproductive system is maturing. Teach the processes factually. Hygiene and products, calmly. Not sexual activity.`,materials:[`Week 28 worksheet`,`Question box`,`Optional: unopened pad (still in wrapper) as a visual — no demo on a body`],knowledge:[`Menstruation is a monthly cycle that prepares the uterus; if an egg is not fertilized, the lining leaves the body as a period.`,`Ovulation is when an ovary releases an egg. Hormones regulate the cycle.`,`Testicles produce sperm. Sperm travels through the vas deferens and is combined with other fluid to produce semen.`,`These processes are signs of reproductive capacity — not a signal to start sexual activity, which is not taught in Grade 5.`],understanding:[`Physical changes during puberty affect reproductive capability. Timing varies. Periods and sperm production can start at different ages. First periods can be irregular.`],skills:[`Describe how physical changes during puberty affect reproductive capability.`,`Describe the processes of menstruation and sperm production.`],warmUp:`Reset the science-voice rule. Today’s words: menstruation, period, ovulation, sperm, semen, hygiene. Giggles get one reset.`,teachTitle:`Two signs of capacity`,teachSteps:[`Menstruation: hormones help the uterus grow a lining. An ovary may release an egg (ovulation). If that egg is not going to become a pregnancy, the lining leaves the body through the vagina — that is a period. Blood and tissue. It can last several days. First periods are often irregular. This is a sign the system is maturing.`,`People who menstruate can use a pad (sits in underwear), a tampon (goes in the vagina — name it, do not practise), or other products families choose. Change them, wash hands, and tell a trusted adult if pain is intense or if they are unprepared at school. Schools have a plan for that — know yours.`,`Sperm production: testicles make sperm cells. Sperm travels through the vas deferens and mixes with fluid to make semen. Erections and wet dreams can happen and are common. They are not something to joke about in the hallway.`,`We name that pregnancy can become possible later when sperm and egg meet. How that happens, implantation, and birth are Grade 6. Grade 5 stops at capacity and the two signs.`,`Everyone in the room learns both systems. This is science, not a boys’ lesson and a girls’ lesson.`],story:`On Tuesday morning, Ms. Chen put two question cards beside the science models. "Use calm, accurate words," she reminded the class. "And remember that learning about a body is never permission to tease someone about it."

Sophie had wondered what she would do if her period started at school. In her group, she explained that menstruation is part of a cycle. During a period, the lining of the uterus leaves the body. It is a normal body process, not something dirty or a punishment. Sophie wrote a plan in her notebook: ask the office, Ms. Chen, or another trusted adult for a pad. She would not have to make a public announcement. The school kept supplies in more than one place.

Ravi read the next card. Sperm is made in the testicles. Semen is sperm mixed with other fluid. Sometimes a person with a penis has a wet dream, which can happen during sleep and is not a joke or a reason to embarrass anyone. Ravi added that washing up and speaking respectfully were sensible responses.

At recess, someone asked why everyone needed to learn both topics. Ms. Chen said classmates might need to be kind and helpful, and any student might grow into a trusted adult for someone else. The lessons also helped everyone understand human biology.

Sophie placed one final card in the question box: "Does being able to produce an egg or sperm mean someone is ready to become a parent?" Ms. Chen answered no. Reproductive capacity is a biological change, not a green light for adult decisions. The class left with facts, privacy, and dignity on the same page.`,activity:`Two-column process notes + hygiene + “everyone learns both.” Private questions go in the box.`,worksheetTitle:`Two signs of capacity`,fields:[{kind:`table`,id:`proc`,columns:[`Process`,`What happens, in Grade 5 language`],rowLabels:[`Menstruation / a period`,`Ovulation (one line)`,`Sperm production / semen`]},{kind:`text`,id:`hygiene`,label:`One hygiene or help step (pad, wash, trusted adult, extra clothes) a person might need`},{kind:`text`,id:`both`,label:`Why does everyone in Grade 5 learn both systems?`},{kind:`exit`,id:`exit`,prompt:`What does “sign of reproductive capacity” mean — and what does it not mean?`}],sample:{proc:`Period: uterus lining leaves the body through the vagina for a few days; first ones can be irregular. | Ovulation: an ovary releases an egg. | Testicles make sperm; sperm plus fluid is semen.`,hygiene:`A pad in underwear, wash hands, extra clothes in a bag, tell a trusted adult or the office if I am unprepared or in a lot of pain.`,both:`These are human body systems. Classmates deserve dignity. You may need to be a respectful friend.`,exit:`It means the system is maturing so pregnancy could become possible later. It does not mean someone is ready to parent, and it is not a lesson on sexual activity.`},checks:[`Both processes stated without slang?`,`Hygiene named without embarrassment theatre?`,`Grade 6 sequence left in Grade 6?`],tip:`Know where pads are in your building before this lesson. Answer question-box items factually next class. Skip anything that is not Grade 5 PEW.`,preview:`Next week: positive health practices during puberty.`},{week:29,unitId:`growth`,title:`Positive Health Practices During Puberty`,focus:`Sleep, food, hygiene, activity, adults`,outcomeFocus:`Positive health practices during puberty include physical activity, nutritious foods, reducing stress, sleep, hygiene, and talking to trusted adults. These support a healthy reproductive system and overall well-being.`,materials:[`Week 29 worksheet`],knowledge:[`Positive health practices during puberty include engaging in physical activity, eating nutritious foods, reducing stress, sleep, hygiene, and connecting with trusted adults.`],understanding:[`Positive health practices during puberty support a healthy reproductive system and well-being.`],skills:[`Identify positive health practices during puberty.`],warmUp:`List five practices on the board: sleep, food, activity, hygiene, trusted adult. Students add reducing stress as a sixth (a walk, a quiet corner, telling someone — not “just calm down”).`,teachTitle:`Practices, not perfection`,teachSteps:[`Sleep: growing bodies often need more. Phones out of the bed if the family can.`,`Food: the same Food Guide pattern. Iron-rich foods matter for some. No shrinking diets.`,`Activity: daily movement supports mood, bones, and the systems we named.`,`Hygiene: deodorant, washing, clean clothes, period products. This is respect, not a popularity contest.`,`Stress: puberty can feel loud. Reducing stress is a health practice — break, talk, move, rest.`,`Trusted adults: questions and worries go to a person, not to a random comment section.`],story:`By Thursday afternoon, Diego felt as if his whole week had been turned up too loud. He had stayed up finishing a game, forgotten his water bottle, and rushed through breakfast. In gym class he was not in trouble, but he was tired and cranky. When Ms. Okafor asked the class to brainstorm health practices during puberty, Diego quietly wrote, "I need a reset."

The class made a realistic plan together. Diego could set an earlier bedtime on school nights, refill his bottle before leaving home, and keep deodorant in his backpack if a trusted adult said that was a good choice. After a noisy day, a walk, stretching, music, or a few quiet minutes might help him feel settled. He also chose his aunt, as well as a parent, as an adult he could talk with when a change or question worried him.

Mina suggested adding "look older" to the list. Diego asked whether that was really a health practice. Ms. Okafor shook her head. The class crossed it out, along with "look smaller." Bodies do not have to reach one particular shape or speed to be healthy, and classmates should never be ranked.

On Friday, Diego tried only two changes: water at lunch and an earlier lights-out time. He still felt tired, but he noticed that the afternoon went more smoothly. He put a question in the class box about choosing hygiene products, knowing Ms. Okafor could answer privately or connect him with a trusted adult. The lesson was not about perfect routines. It was about small, supportive choices for a changing body.`,activity:`Build a personal practice card — six small, realistic items. No sharing of private hygiene details out loud.`,worksheetTitle:`Practices that support a maturing body`,fields:[{kind:`table`,id:`pract`,columns:[`Practice`,`What this could look like for me this month`],rowLabels:[`Sleep`,`Nutritious food / water`,`Physical activity`,`Hygiene`,`Reducing stress`,`Trusted adult`]},{kind:`exit`,id:`exit`,prompt:`How do positive health practices support a healthy reproductive system — in one kid sentence?`}],sample:{pract:`Sleep: phone out of bed when I can. Food: water + a protein-and-fruit snack after gym. Activity: intramurals twice a week. Hygiene: deodorant in the backpack. Stress: a walk after a loud day. Adult: parent and my teacher.`,exit:`Sleep, food, movement, hygiene, and less stress help the whole body, including the systems that are maturing, work the way they are supposed to.`},checks:[`Six practices attempted?`,`No body-ranking language?`],tip:`Some students cannot control bedtime or food at home. Keep items in the student’s circle of control. School water and school movement count.`,preview:`Next week: connecting puberty to well-being — unit wrap.`},{week:30,unitId:`growth`,title:`Connecting Puberty to Well-Being`,focus:`Unit wrap`,outcomeFocus:`Pull both outcomes together: investigate maturation, and connect puberty to reproductive capacity — with respect, accurate language, and health practices. Still no Grade 6 sequence.`,materials:[`Week 30 worksheet`,`Unit rubric`,`Question box (last pass)`],knowledge:[`Well-being during adolescence is supported by accurate knowledge, respectful participation, and positive health practices.`],understanding:[`Knowledge of maturation and reproductive capacity supports well-being when it is factual and respectful.`],skills:[`Investigate maturation and identify changes during adolescence.`,`Connect puberty to the capacity for human reproduction.`],warmUp:`Three sentences on the board to finish: Puberty is… Capacity means… I will not…`,teachTitle:`What we taught — and what we did not`,teachSteps:[`We taught: maturation; physical, cognitive, and behavioural changes; puberty and hormones; secondary sex characteristics; reproductive organs and functions; menstruation and sperm production as signs of capacity; positive health practices.`,`We did not teach: the fertilization-through-pregnancy-and-birth sequence (Grade 6); sexual activity; contraception; dating advice.`,`Gender identity lessons are not part of this unit plan — stay on the PEW growth outcomes named above.`,`Pass was allowed. Question box was for facts, not for personal stories about people in the room.`],story:`For the last puberty lesson, Mr. Singh taped a long strip of paper across the classroom wall. One end said "What we learned," and the other said "What we did not learn." Students carried their notes from the earlier lessons and placed them where they belonged.

Ravi wrote that puberty is a set of physical changes as a body moves toward adult reproductive capacity. Later, a body may be able to produce egg or sperm cells. He underlined the word later. Being able to produce those cells does not mean a young person is ready to parent, make every adult decision, or be treated like an adult.

Sophie added that people's bodies change at different times and in different ways. She had heard classmates use slang to compare bodies, but she decided her own rule would be simple: no ranking, guessing, or jokes about someone's body. If a question felt private, she could use the anonymous question box or speak with a parent, teacher, school nurse, counsellor, or another trusted adult.

On the other side of the strip, the class placed sleep, food, movement, hygiene, water, and asking for help. These habits could support well-being, but they were not tests that anyone had to pass perfectly.

Mr. Singh read the finished wall aloud. "Science gives us information," he said. "Respect tells us how to use it." Ravi noticed that the two sections connected: knowing the facts could help someone make a plan, while protecting privacy could help people feel safe. Before dismissal, each student wrote one sentence to remember. Sophie's said, "A changing body deserves accurate information and kindness." The class agreed that was a strong ending to the unit.`,activity:`Unit wrap paragraph + self-check against the rubric. Remaining question-box items answered factually if they are Grade 5 PEW.`,worksheetTitle:`Growth wrap`,fields:[{kind:`text`,id:`puberty`,label:`Puberty is… (one accurate sentence)`},{kind:`text`,id:`capacity`,label:`Capacity for human reproduction means… (and does not mean…)`},{kind:`text`,id:`change`,label:`One physical, one thinking, and one behaviour change`},{kind:`text`,id:`practice`,label:`Two positive health practices I can keep`},{kind:`exit`,id:`exit`,prompt:`How does accurate, respectful knowledge about puberty support well-being?`}],sample:{puberty:`Puberty is the set of physical changes, driven by hormones, that lead toward adult reproductive capacity. Timing varies.`,capacity:`It means the body is becoming able to produce egg or sperm cells. It does not mean someone is ready to parent, and it is not a lesson on sexual activity or birth.`,change:`Physical: growth spurt. Thinking: caring more about a group project. Behaviour: wanting more privacy.`,practice:`Water bottle and deodorant in the bag; talk to a trusted adult instead of a comment section.`,exit:`Facts replace rumours. Respect replaces ranking. People can ask for help. That supports well-being during a stage that already feels loud.`},checks:[`Accurate language?`,`Capacity vs ready?`,`No Grade 6 content sneaking in?`],tip:`Score the growth rubric once. Keep returned family-letter slips in a folder. Students who opted out should already have been on the alternate task — never a public list.`,preview:`Weeks 31–36: Financial Literacy — needs, wants, saving, budgets, choices. Sample dollars only.`}],a=[{week:31,unitId:`financial`,title:`Needs and Wants`,focus:`What money is for`,outcomeFocus:`Needs are required for well-being (food, shelter, clothing, safety). Wants are extra. Both are human. Planning starts by telling them apart.`,materials:[`Week 31 worksheet`],knowledge:[`A need supports health and safety. A want is nice-to-have.`,`Context matters: running shoes can be a need for gym and a want if they must be a $250 brand.`],understanding:[`Telling needs from wants is the first planning skill. Families have different needs. We do not rank anyone’s house, lunch, or phone.`],skills:[`Distinguish needs and wants as a foundation for financial planning.`],warmUp:`Sort: water, video game, winter coat, extra hoodie, bus fare, concert merch. Two disagreements — talk them out.`,teachTitle:`Needs, wants, and grey zones`,teachSteps:[`A need supports health and safety. A want is nice-to-have.`,`Context matters: running shoes can be a need for gym and a want if they must be a $250 brand.`,`Families have different needs. We do not rank anyone’s house, lunch, or phone.`,`Telling them apart is the first planning skill. You can still enjoy a want — after the needs are covered, or with a plan.`],story:`On a snowy Monday in Edmonton, Hana emptied a pretend wallet onto her desk. The class was sorting picture cards: water, a video game, a winter coat, an extra hoodie, bus fare, and concert merchandise. Jack immediately placed the extra hoodie with wants. Hana paused. Her family's only warm coat had a broken zipper, and an extra layer could matter on a cold walk home.

Ms. Patel asked them to explain instead of arguing. A winter coat might be a need because it supports health and safety. A second hoodie with a ninety-dollar logo was more likely a want. Running shoes could be a need for gym class, while a much more expensive brand might be a want if a less costly pair worked safely. Context mattered.

The class then imagined receiving twenty dollars. Lunch would come first if it was not already provided. Bus fare could be important for getting home. A helmet would be a need for safe cycling. Movie snacks, limited-edition cards, and concert shirts could be wants: enjoyable extras, but not required for safety.

Hana noticed that the categories were not insults. Wanting something did not make a person selfish, and needing something did not make it boring. The labels were simply tools for making a plan. She moved the hoodie card to a middle space and wrote, "Ask: Is this for warmth, or for a style I like?"

No one had to share family income or explain private choices. The students used sample dollars only. Before packing up, Jack said he would ask what a purchase was meant to do before deciding. Hana tucked the cards away, ready to remember that needs and wants can be discussed with care, especially when the answer depends on the situation.`,activity:`Sort a list, then label one personal example of each without sharing money amounts out loud.`,worksheetTitle:`Need or want?`,fields:[{kind:`table`,id:`sort`,columns:[`Item`,`Need, want, or depends?`,`Why`],rowLabels:[`Lunch for school`,`Limited-edition cards`,`Helmet for biking`,`Brand-name hoodie`,`Phone charger`,`Movie snacks`]},{kind:`text`,id:`need`,label:`One need in my life`},{kind:`text`,id:`want`,label:`One want I can wait for`},{kind:`exit`,id:`exit`,prompt:`Why does mixing up needs and wants make planning harder?`}],sample:{sort:`Lunch: need. Cards: want. Helmet: need (safety). Brand hoodie: depends (clothing vs logo). Charger: depends (reach a parent vs extra). Movie snacks: want.`,need:`Winter boots that still fit.`,want:`A new pencil case with a character on it.`,exit:`The want can empty the jar before the boots are covered. You can still enjoy a want after needs are covered, or with a plan.`},checks:[`Can they explain a “depends” item?`,`No shaming of what others have?`],tip:`Some students have almost no spending money. Use school examples (choosing a free activity vs a paid one) so everyone can play.`,preview:`Next: saving and goals — short-term and long-term.`},{week:32,unitId:`financial`,title:`Saving and Financial Goals`,focus:`Short-term and long-term`,outcomeFocus:`Saving means not spending now so money is there for a goal or an unexpected expense. Short-term goals can support long-term ones.`,materials:[`Week 32 worksheet`,`Calculator optional`],knowledge:[`Short-term goals: days to a few months. Long-term goals: a year or more.`,`Saving strategies: name the number and the time, skip some wants, put money aside on purpose (jar, envelope, account with a family adult).`],understanding:[`A short-term save helps a long-term goal because the habit is the same skill.`],skills:[`Set short-term and long-term financial goals and plan a simple save-up.`],warmUp:`Goal: $40 soccer registration in 8 weeks. How much per week if they save evenly? ($5) What if they also want a $10 snack this weekend?`,teachTitle:`Goals need a number and a time`,teachSteps:[`Short-term: days to a few months (a book, a field trip fee, a gift).`,`Long-term: a year or more, usually more money, needs commitment (a bike, a camp, helping family with a bigger item).`,`Saving strategies: decide the goal, skip some wants, put money aside on purpose (jar, envelope, account with a family adult).`,`Unexpected expenses are why a small buffer helps. You will not predict everything.`],story:`Mason brought a folded paper to the lunchroom table. On the front he had drawn a soccer ball and written, "Forty dollars in eight weeks for registration." He felt proud of the goal until he remembered the ten-dollar snack he wanted for Saturday's movie. His face tightened. It seemed as if one choice had to defeat the other.

In class, Ms. Bouchard helped him turn the goal into numbers. Forty dollars divided across eight weeks meant saving five dollars each week. Mason could ask his family about an allowance or a paid household task, but the example did not promise that money would appear. He also listed the snack as a want and considered waiting, choosing a smaller treat, or using money that was left after saving.

His partner, Leila, had a different goal. She wanted a sixteen-dollar set of used chapter books in four weeks. Her plan was four dollars a week. She also dreamed of saving sixty dollars over twelve weeks for a used bike helmet and lights, which meant five dollars a week. The larger goal felt less mysterious once she broke it into weekly steps.

Mason decided to test a short-term plan first. For the next four weeks, he would place four dollars in a labelled envelope for the books he wanted to give his cousin and keep one dollar for a small treat. He would talk with his family before changing the plan.

The class learned that saving was not about never spending. It was putting money aside on purpose for something that mattered. A small goal could build the habit needed for a longer goal. Mason left still hoping for popcorn, but now he had a number, a time, and a choice instead of a wish floating around in his head.`,activity:`Write one short-term and one long-term goal with a simple save-up math box. Keep dollar amounts modest and fictional if needed.`,worksheetTitle:`Two goals, one saving plan`,fields:[{kind:`table`,id:`goals`,columns:[``,`Short-term goal`,`Long-term goal`],rowLabels:[`What I would save for`,`About how much ($)`,`How many weeks`,`Money to set aside each week`]},{kind:`text`,id:`pause`,label:`One want I would pause to hit the short-term goal`},{kind:`exit`,id:`exit`,prompt:`How can a short-term save (habit) help a long-term goal?`}],sample:{goals:`Short: used chapter-book set, $16, 4 weeks, $4/week. Long: used helmet and lights, $60, 12 weeks, $5/week.`,pause:`Extra vending popcorn this weekend.`,exit:`The habit — putting money aside on purpose — is the same skill. A smaller number first makes the bigger goal more possible.`},checks:[`Both goals have a time and a rough amount?`,`Division attempted (even if you help)?`],tip:`If math stalls the 12 minutes, give the weekly amount and have them check it. The Health idea is the plan, not long division.`,preview:`Next week we put this into a simple budget.`},{week:33,unitId:`financial`,title:`Simple Budgets`,focus:`Income, expenses, leftover`,outcomeFocus:`A budget is a plan for earning, spending, saving, and donating over a period. Saving is a choice in the plan, not a leftover that maybe happens.`,materials:[`Week 33 worksheet`],knowledge:[`A budget includes money on hand or coming in, money going out, and money set aside.`,`Needs first, then wants. If expenses beat income, cut a want or add time to the goal.`],understanding:[`Planning supports financial goals when the numbers actually balance.`],skills:[`Build a simple budget that includes needs, wants, and saving.`],warmUp:`Show a 3-line budget on the board: In $20 (birthday + chores). Needs $8. Wants $6. Save $6. Ask: is this a plan or a wish?`,teachTitle:`Build a tiny budget`,teachSteps:[`Income: gift, chore pay, allowance — whatever is real for that student, or use the class sample numbers. Never a required family income.`,`Expenses: needs first, then wants.`,`Saving and donating are choices in the plan, not leftovers that “maybe” happen.`,`If expenses beat income, cut a want or add time to the goal. Do not pretend.`],story:`Aaliyah was planning a class celebration when she discovered that twenty dollars could disappear quickly. She wanted a snack, a craft kit, and a new pencil case, but she also hoped to save for a class T-shirt. Ms. Roy gave everyone a sample budget sheet with three lines: money in, money out, and money left for later.

Aaliyah wrote twenty dollars in the first line. She put eight dollars beside needs, including a bus and snack fund. Six dollars went beside wants. The final six dollars went into savings. Ms. Roy asked, "Is this a plan or just a wish?"

Aaliyah checked the numbers. Eight plus six plus six equalled twenty, so every dollar had a job. She imagined spending the whole amount on the craft kit before the month ended. That would feel exciting on Tuesday, but it would leave no money for the bus, snacks, or her goal. She decided to wait on the kit and keep the six dollars saved.

For another example, the class used thirty dollars for one month of fun and saving. Ten dollars covered needs, such as bus fare and a snack fund. Twelve dollars was set aside for a T-shirt goal. That left eight dollars for wants. If a student wanted a more expensive treat, something in the wants line would have to shrink. Pretending the totals balanced would not solve the problem.

Aaliyah liked that a budget was not a punishment or a complicated adult document. It was a small plan that showed what money could do. At home, she would ask which costs her family already covered before using the sample numbers. She circled "check, then choose" at the bottom of her page and carried the idea into the next lesson.`,activity:`Budget using the sample numbers on the sheet. Then one personal or sample weekly budget.`,worksheetTitle:`A simple budget`,fields:[{kind:`intro`,text:`Sample: You have $30 for a month of “fun + save.” Goal: $12 saved for a class t-shirt. Needs (bus/snack fund) $10. How much is left for wants?`},{kind:`table`,id:`budget`,columns:[`Budget part`,`Amount ($)`,`Need, want, save, or donate?`],rowLabels:[`Money I have or will get`,`Need 1`,`Want 1`,`Save`,`Donate (optional)`]},{kind:`choice`,id:`balance`,label:`Does it balance (in = out + save)?`,options:[`Yes`,`Not yet — I would change a want`]},{kind:`exit`,id:`exit`,prompt:`What is a budget, in one sentence?`}],sample:{budget:`In $30. Need $10 (bus/snack). Want $8. Save $12 (t-shirt). Donate $0. 30 = 10+8+12.`,balance:`Yes`,exit:`A budget is a plan for money coming in and going out — including saving on purpose, not as a leftover.`},checks:[`Needs before wants?`,`Saving is a line, not an accident?`],tip:`Always offer sample dollars. Never require a real family income.`,preview:`Next week: consumer choices — what influences us.`},{week:34,unitId:`financial`,title:`Consumer Choices — What Influences Us`,focus:`Ads, friends, price, quality`,outcomeFocus:`Examine factors that influence consumer choice: advertising, peers, price, quality, packaging, and feelings. Planning includes noticing those factors.`,materials:[`Week 34 worksheet`,`Optional: one printed ad with the brand name covered`],knowledge:[`Consumer choices are influenced by ads, friends, price, quality, packaging, habit, and feelings (boredom, belonging).`,`A want can be dressed up as a need by an ad.`],understanding:[`Noticing influence is part of planning. You can still buy a want — with a plan, not on autopilot.`],skills:[`Examine factors that influence consumer choice.`],warmUp:`Show an ad (or describe one): bright colours, a kid laughing, “limited time.” Ask: what is the product doing, and what is the ad doing?`,teachTitle:`Influence is not the same as a need`,teachSteps:[`Price: what it costs. Quality: how long it lasts or how well it works. They are not always the same.`,`Ads: colour, music, famous people, countdown timers. Their job is to get a yes.`,`Peers: belonging can feel like a purchase. That is a real feeling. It is still a want until you check.`,`Packaging and placement (eye-level, checkout) are designed. You can notice them.`,`A pause question: Do I need this, want this, or was I just asked very loudly?`],story:`At lunch, Aaliyah saw a bright phone advertisement for limited-edition cards. The words LAST CHANCE flashed above a picture of a shiny pack. Two students at her table had bought some, and they invited her to join their collection. Aaliyah wanted the cards immediately. She had eight dollars in her pocket, the same eight dollars marked for snacks and savings.

In class, Mr. Laurent asked everyone to slow down and name the influences around the decision. Friends made the cards feel like a way to belong. The advertisement created urgency. The price was eight dollars. Aaliyah thought about quality too: she had enjoyed similar cards for a week, then left them in a drawer.

Writing down the influences did not make her foolish for wanting the pack. It simply gave her more information. Mr. Laurent suggested a waiting plan. Aaliyah could leave the money where it was for one week, check her budget, and ask whether she still wanted the cards after the "last chance" feeling had faded.

The following Friday, the cards were still on her mind, but her budget had changed. She had a library trip and needed a snack, and she wanted to keep part of the money for her T-shirt goal. She decided not to buy the pack yet. Her friends were surprised, so she explained that waiting was not saying the cards were bad. It was helping her choose on purpose.

Aaliyah learned that ads, friends, price, and quality could all influence a choice. None of them could decide for her. If she had waited and still had money after needs and goals were covered, she could have bought the cards without guilt. For now, her jar held eight dollars, and that felt like a choice she could see.`,activity:`Take one real or sample want. Name the influences. Decide: buy now, wait, or skip — and why.`,worksheetTitle:`What is pulling on this choice?`,fields:[{kind:`text`,id:`item`,label:`One item (sample is fine — do not name what a classmate owns)`},{kind:`checks`,id:`factors`,label:`Influences I notice (check all that apply)`,options:[`Advertising / “limited time”`,`Friends or belonging`,`Price (it seems cheap or it seems expensive)`,`Quality / how long it lasts`,`Packaging or being at checkout`,`Feeling (bored, stressed, left out)`]},{kind:`text`,id:`needwant`,label:`Is this a need, a want, or a want dressed up as a need? Why?`},{kind:`choice`,id:`decide`,label:`My plan`,options:[`Buy now (needs covered, in the budget)`,`Wait one week`,`Skip`]},{kind:`exit`,id:`exit`,prompt:`Why does noticing an influence help a financial goal?`}],sample:{item:`Limited-edition cards, $8, “last chance” ad.`,factors:`Advertising / “limited time”; Friends or belonging; Price (it seems cheap or it seems expensive)`,needwant:`A want dressed up as a need by belonging and a countdown. I do not need them to be well.`,decide:`Wait one week`,exit:`If I buy on autopilot, the want can empty the jar before the goal. Noticing lets me choose on purpose.`},checks:[`More than one influence named?`,`Decision connected to a plan, not to shame?`],tip:`Do not roast anyone’s shoes or lunch. Use sample products.`,preview:`Next week: earn, spend, save, donate — four money jobs.`},{week:35,unitId:`financial`,title:`Planning: Earn, Spend, Save, Donate`,focus:`Four money jobs`,outcomeFocus:`Planning can include four jobs for money: earn, spend, save, donate. Not every student will do all four this month. The skill is to put them in a plan on purpose.`,materials:[`Week 35 worksheet`],knowledge:[`Earning for Grade 5 might be chores, gifts, a small paid task with a family adult — never required work that replaces rest or school.`,`Spending includes needs and wants. Saving is for goals and surprises. Donating is a choice to contribute (connects to Week 1 volunteering — time is also a donation).`],understanding:[`A plan that names all four jobs (even if donate is $0 this month) is more complete than a plan that only spends.`],skills:[`Demonstrate how planning can support financial goals.`],warmUp:`Four corners: earn, spend, save, donate. Students walk to the job they think about most. Then to the job they think about least.`,teachTitle:`Four jobs, one plan`,teachSteps:[`Earn: money in. Keep examples Grade 5 and optional. Gifts count. So does a sample number.`,`Spend: needs first, then planned wants.`,`Save: the goal from Week 32, plus a tiny buffer if possible.`,`Donate: money, or time/talent/energy (Week 1). Both are contribution. Nobody is a better person for a bigger number.`,`If a student has $0, they can still plan with sample dollars and they can still donate time.`],story:`Mason drew four boxes on a sheet of blue paper and labelled them earn, spend, save, and donate. "That sounds like a lot for one week," he said. Ms. Bouchard explained that the boxes were four possible jobs for money or time, not four rules that had to receive equal amounts.

Mason's sample week began with five dollars from a family task. He planned to spend two dollars on a small want, save three dollars toward his helmet and bike lights, and donate time by helping the school's lost-and-found team for twenty minutes. He had been tempted by vending-machine popcorn, but putting the want in the plan helped him avoid an accidental purchase. This week, his donate-money box stayed at zero. His time still counted.

Hana used a different sample. She did not have earning money that month, so she wrote ten dollars in the example column rather than pretending she had a job. Four dollars went to a bus need, six dollars went to savings, and zero dollars went to a money donation. She planned to help the recycling crew after school instead.

Mason asked whether donating time was less important than donating money. The class decided that helping, sharing useful items when a family agreed, and giving money were different ways to contribute. A person's plan depended on what they had available.

At the end, the students checked that their choices fit their real or sample amounts. Mason still had a want, a goal, and a helpful task ahead. Hana still had a goal without claiming an income she did not have. The four boxes were not a contest. They were a way to notice choices and make money serve a purpose.`,activity:`Fill four jobs for a sample week. At least one line must be real-or-sample save. Donate may be time.`,worksheetTitle:`Four jobs for money (and time)`,fields:[{kind:`table`,id:`jobs`,columns:[`Job`,`What this looks like this month (sample dollars are fine)`],rowLabels:[`Earn`,`Spend (need)`,`Spend (want, if any)`,`Save`,`Donate (money or time)`]},{kind:`text`,id:`link`,label:`How does this plan support one financial goal I named in Week 32 or 33?`,rows:2},{kind:`exit`,id:`exit`,prompt:`Why can donating time count in a financial-literacy plan in Grade 5?`}],sample:{jobs:`Earn: sample $5 family task. Spend need: $2 bus. Spend want: $0 this week. Save: $3 helmet. Donate: 20 minutes lost-and-found (time).`,link:`The $3 a week is the habit toward the $60 helmet. The want is paused. Time-donate keeps contribution in the plan even at $0.`,exit:`Not every student has money to give. Time, talent, and energy are contribution (Week 1) and they belong in a well-being plan.`},checks:[`Save is a line?`,`Donate allowed to be time?`,`No required family income?`],tip:`If a student is proud of a large donate number from a family, accept it without turning it into a class contest.`,preview:`Last week: my financial plan — unit wrap.`},{week:36,unitId:`financial`,title:`My Financial Plan`,focus:`Unit wrap`,outcomeFocus:`Students demonstrate how planning can support financial goals. One page: needs/wants, a goal, a budget, one influence they will notice, four jobs.`,materials:[`Week 36 worksheet`,`Unit rubric`],knowledge:[`Planning supports financial goals when needs and wants are named, a goal has a number and a time, a budget balances, and influences are noticed.`],understanding:[`Informed financial decision making contributes to well-being — with sample dollars, without ranking families.`],skills:[`Demonstrate how planning can support financial goals.`],warmUp:`One-minute list: need, want, short-term goal, one influence ads use. Four words on paper. Then build the plan around them.`,teachTitle:`The plan is the outcome`,teachSteps:[`A proficient wrap has: a need vs a want, a goal with $ and weeks, a budget that balances on sample or real small numbers, one consumer influence, and a next step.`,`Well-being connection: less worry, a goal that moves, contribution if they chose it — not “being rich.”`,`Score the unit rubric once. Proficient is the target.`],story:`For the final financial-literacy activity, Aaliyah spread a sheet of paper across her desk. At the top she wrote, "A plan I can explain." She used sample dollars, because the class never needed to share private family information.

Her first line was a need: bus fare. Her want was a pack of cards, but she wrote "wait one week" beside it. Her goal was a twelve-dollar class T-shirt in four weeks. Three dollars each week would reach the goal, as long as she had money available and checked the plan with her family.

Aaliyah then tried a monthly sample budget with twenty dollars coming in. Eight dollars went to needs. Zero dollars went to wants for that month, because she chose to pause the cards. Twelve dollars went into the T-shirt goal. The numbers balanced, and the goal had both a number and a time.

In the corner, she listed an influence she wanted to notice: advertisements that said "last chance." If one appeared, she would wait, name the feeling it created, and look at her budget before spending. For the donate line, she chose a recycling-crew shift at school. She might not have money to give, but she could give time with permission.

When Ms. Roy asked how planning supported the goal, Aaliyah pointed to the page. "The money has jobs," she said. "The want is paused on purpose, and I can see what might pull me away." She knew the plan might need to change if a real need came up. Changing it would mean checking the numbers again, not giving up.

Aaliyah folded the page and placed it in her notebook. The final lesson was not that every choice would be perfect. It was that noticing needs, wants, influences, goals, and available money could help her make a thoughtful choice one small step at a time.`,activity:`One-page plan. Optional share of the goal line only — no family money out loud.`,worksheetTitle:`My Grade 5 financial plan`,fields:[{kind:`text`,id:`need`,label:`One need I will cover first`},{kind:`text`,id:`want`,label:`One want I can wait for (or plan for)`},{kind:`text`,id:`goal`,label:`One goal: what, about how much, how many weeks, weekly save`},{kind:`text`,id:`budget`,label:`A tiny budget that balances (in = needs + wants + save + donate)`},{kind:`text`,id:`influence`,label:`One influence I will notice before I buy`},{kind:`exit`,id:`exit`,prompt:`How does planning support a financial goal — in one sentence?`}],sample:{need:`Bus / snack fund.`,want:`Limited-edition cards — wait one week.`,goal:`Class t-shirt, $12, 4 weeks, $3 a week.`,budget:`In $20. Need $8. Want $0 this month. Save $12. Donate $0 + recycling time. Balances.`,influence:`“Last chance” ads and belonging at the table.`,exit:`A goal with a number and a time, needs covered first, and wants chosen on purpose — so the jar is not empty by accident.`},checks:[`Goal has a number and a time?`,`Budget attempted?`,`No family-income sharing?`],tip:`Celebrate the plan, not the dollar size. A $4 field-trip fee is as valid as a $60 helmet.`,preview:`Year complete. Six units, thirty-six lessons, one 30-minute block at a time.`}],o=[...e,...t,...n,...r,...i,...a],s=Object.fromEntries(o.map(e=>[e.week,e]));function c(e){return o.filter(t=>t.unitId===e)}export{s as n,c as r,o as t};